<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aarav Khanna
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>          <span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BASIS Phoenix
<br class="ltx_break"></span>          Phoenix, AZ, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Maanak Gupta
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Department of Computer Science</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tennessee Tech University
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Cookeville, TN, USA
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">While the widespread deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) holds great potential for society, their vulnerabilities to adversarial manipulation and exploitation can pose serious safety, security, and ethical risks. As new threats continue to emerge, it becomes critically necessary to assess the landscape of LLMs’ safety and security against evolving adversarial prompt techniques. To understand the behavior of LLMs, this research provides an empirical analysis and risk profile of nine prominent LLMs — Claude Opus 4, DeepSeek V3 (both open-source and online), Gemini 2.5 Flash, GPT-4o, Grok 3, Llama 4 Scout, Mistral 7B, and Qwen 3 1.7B — against 24 different security and safety categories.
These LLMs are evaluated on their ability to produce harmful responses for adversarially crafted prompts (dataset has been made public) for a broad range of <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">safety</span> and <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">security topics</span>, such as <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">promotion of violent criminal behavior</span>, <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">promotion of non-violent criminal activity</span>, <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">societal harms related to safety</span>, <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">illegal sexual content</span>, <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dangerous code generation</span>, and <span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cybersecurity threats beyond code</span>. Our study introduces the Risk Severity Index (RSI), an agile and scalable evaluation score, to quantify and compare the security posture and creating a risk profile of LLMs.
As the LLM development landscape progresses, the RSI is intended to be a valuable metric for comparing the risks of LLMs across evolving threats. This research finds widespread vulnerabilities in the safety filters of the LLMs tested and highlights the urgent need for stronger alignment, responsible deployment practices, and model governance, particularly for open-access and rapidly iterated models.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Large Language Models, Artificial Intelligence, Generative AI, Safety and Security Analysis, Risk Profiling, Vulnerability Study

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen AI) continues to evolve rapidly and shape the landscapes of its application domains, including healthcare, education, and finance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. One of the most prominent and widely used forms of generative AI today is the Large Language Model (LLM), which uses natural language capabilities to assist humans across a variety of language-focused use cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The release of OpenAI’s ChatGPT, an interface for everyday users to interact with OpenAI’s LLMs, in 2022 continued a race among companies trying to develop and release LLMs of their own <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Today, many prominent LLMs exist in the AI industry, such as Google with its Gemini line of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, Meta with its Llama series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, xAI with its Grok models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and DeepSeek with its highly proficient V3 and R1 models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In education, LLMs can benefit students by serving as teaching assistants, providing students with supplementary insight into specific topics, and translating learning materials for students to better understand <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Specifically focused models like MathAgent for mathematical problems have even been created to streamline these use cases. In healthcare, LLMs currently provide diagnoses and treatment options with an ever-increasing degree of accuracy and a low risk of harmful advice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Meanwhile, LLMs are used to analyze financial data and stay ahead of market trends <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, as LLMs become more accessible to everyday users, they also become more exposed to the risk of adversarial misuse. These tools also serve as an increasingly enticing vector for malicious actors to exploit for a variety of illegal and immoral purposes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and the susceptibility of current LLMs to misuse, adversarial attacks, and safety issues highlights the critical need for thorough assessments of their potential harms.
Insufficient implementations of LLM’s safety filters can have sweeping harms. While general use LLMs may provide responses that aid threat actors in cyberattacks or violent crime, specialized jailbroken models that answer any question given to them, regardless of morality or ethics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Anthropic’s August 2025 Threat Intelligence Report demonstrates various current misuses of LLMs such as Claude being used to aid threat actors in romance scams <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, Trend Micro reports an increase in criminals jailbreaking LLMs to create malicious scripts that evade antivirus signatures. Moreover, the OWASP Foundation labels prompt injection vulnerabilities, where attackers manipulate or reword inputs to achieve harmful or unintended outcomes, as the number one risk to LLMs in 2025 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. As risks continue to emerge, it becomes critically necessary to assess the current landscape of LLM safety and risk against adversarial prompt techniques. To highlight some of the AI risks, the US National Institute for Standards and Technology released its <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI Risk Management Framework</span> (AI RMF 1.0) in 2023 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. The framework provides a structured approach for addressing risk management of AI being deployed and used within an organization. The framework emphasizes trustworthiness of AI models which can be judged by characteristics like being privacy-enhanced and accountable/reliable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Prior research works have evaluated the safety and misuse potential of LLMs through a variety of frameworks, such as HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, SAGE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, or REVEAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Tests of guardrails are often categorized by the type of harm that a failed or missing safety guardrail could allow the model to produce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. These harm categories are succinctly broken down into three types in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>: sexual harm, violence harm, and misinformation harm. Most of the current frameworks for model alignment and safety evaluations deliver an emphasis on multi-turn attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, where harmful outputs are extracted from the LLM using a conversational method that typically begins with less harmful conversation and gradually shifts to a more harmful focus. Further, other evaluations have conducted safety evaluations that focus on one or few LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In our research study, inspired by the NIST AI Risk framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, we provide evidence-based data to support the analysis of current LLM risks and test their safety and security against adversarial prompts. We empirically show that vulnerabilities in AI content filtering systems are prevalent and that methods for abusing these vulnerabilities are effective and transferable between different LLMs. Our research examines a broad range of <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">safety</span> and <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">security topics</span>, categorized into: (i) <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">promotion of violent criminal behavior</span>, (ii) <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">promotion of non-violent criminal activity</span>, (iii) <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">societal harms related to safety</span>, (iv) <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">illegal sexual content</span>, (v) <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dangerous code generation</span>, (vi) <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cybersecurity threats beyond code</span>, and (vii) <span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">special interests</span>. Each of these harm topics encompasses a group of categories, that organize harms and allow for a particular (by category) or more generalized (by harm topic) evaluation. By proposing specific and a larger number of categories, we aim to provide a comprehensive and realistic evaluation of the landscape of LLMs safety and security threats today. We compare the effectiveness of nine widely used LLMs accessed in different mediums, as shown in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="TABLE 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, including the Claude Opus 4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, DeepSeek V3 (online and open-source) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, GPT-4o<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, Gemini 2.5 Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, Grok 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, Llama 4 Scout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Mistral 7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and Qwen 3 1.7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, against our designed adversarial crafted prompts covering different harm topics. Some of the example prompts in our evaluation can be found in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and detailed in the paper. One of the challenges noted in NIST report <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> is the difficulty in measuring the risk associated with LLMs. To address this, we introduce the Risk Severity Index (RSI) which will compare the security postures of different models by quantifying reactions to adversarial prompts, and provide harm potential.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10655v1/LLMMisuseScenarios.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/219;" width="476" height="219" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Real LLM misuse cases tested in our empirical study.</figcaption>
</figure>
