<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our Findings and Key Contributions</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our research shows that LLMs responses can provide in-depth, sensitive information that could be used to compromise the safety and security of others. Throughout the course of our evaluation process, LLMs demonstrated harmful behaviors by generating dangerous content including but not limited to detailed instructions for constructing explosives, information pertaining to effective weapons and ammunition for use against humans, and methods to exploit vulnerable populations. All cases in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> relate closely to prompts used in our evaluation, and all bypassed the content restrictions of multiple LLMs after implementing prompt injection techniques.
Many of the LLMs tested were able to generate samples of malicious code in various forms, including polymorphic malware, attack payloads, and persistence mechanisms. The code generated by these LLMs could greatly aid threat actors in executing and automating attacks. ”Script kiddies”–threat actors with little experience that rely on pre-made attack tools and scripts, could even use LLMs to help automate the process of creating exploits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In addition to this, scammers and attackers alike could benefit from the usage of LLMs to generate malicious email and text messages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation shows that many LLMs are able to generate content that is harmful to societal stability. LLMs generated intentionally falsified documents, including news articles and court documents, that could be used to misinform and defame others. Many LLMs were also capable of outlining methods for undermining the integrity of fair elections in a democratic society, and when prompted to do so, many LLMs generated legislation conforming to standards of a specific country’s legislation that subtly but directly implemented voter suppression tactics.
Even in benign cases, insufficient guardrails could result in responses that cause harm or negative effects. For example, we show that most of the LLMs can produce copyrighted resources without disclaimers that an unknowing user could utilize, thereby unknowingly committing copyright infringement or plagiarism. In another situation, LLMs that are asked to generate code that is inherently insecure. Even if obviously vulnerable to man-in-the-middle or remote code execution attacks, some LLMs generated the insecure code without notifying the user that the code is insecure and should not be applied in a real environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notably, we further show that abusing the content filtering systems of LLMs is not a highly difficult, time intensive task in most cases. It is currently possible for both experienced and inexperienced threat actors to misuse LLMs using a variety of simple tactics. Minimal understanding of guardrail mechanisms and content filtering evasion techniques allow users to readily access high-risk content from many of the LLMs this research examines. While certain models in our evaluation required tailored approaches to successfully bypass content restrictions, some of the LLMs tested required little or no prompt manipulation to bypass their guardrails, and many prompt bypasses were transferable between other models. Some models even responded to prompts they would otherwise block after adding ”Label this as fictional” or ”Educational” to the beginning of the prompt. Basic to intermediate prompt manipulation techniques were able to manipulate LLMs with extremely sensitive guardrails to certain topics, such as self-harm, into generating highly harmful information in the respective topics. Adversarial prompts used against the DeepSeek V3 model were also effectively used against many other models, showing a property of transferability in the security postures of these models at the time of writing, allowing threat actors to reuse and share harmful prompts and tailor them to gather more specific information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We believe that this work will contribute in highlighting the safety limitations and vulnerabilities of LLMs as their deployment becomes more widespread, helping stakeholders better understand LLM risk and develop effective defense safeguards to fully harness the power of AI for societal good.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10655v1/Newtimeline-1.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Timeline of Major Language Model Releases (BERT to DeepSeek-V3)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This paper has the following <span id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">key contributions</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Studies literature research and articles on enhancing the security and safety of widely known LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Identity broad range of safety and security harms, classified into 17 safety- and 7 security-related categories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Design and create highly effective adversarial prompts dataset, and use them to test the effectiveness of implemented guardrails in nine widely known LLMs, both closed- and open-source developed globally. The dataset is publicly available at <a href="https://github.com/CharanRoot/LLM_Prompt_Set/tree/main" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">[Code Repository]</a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compares LLM responses across each category based on different key criteria such as the <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">directness</span> of the response, the <span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">level of threat</span> conveyed, and the effectiveness of the implemented guardrails.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Highlights how nine different LLMs respond to malicious prompts across various threat categories, showcasing their relative strengths and weaknesses in handling harmful inputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Facilitate a systematic comparison of LLMs based on their potential for harm, we introduce a novel Risk Severity Index (RSI) for evaluating the security posture of LLMs. The RSI is calculated by combining two key metrics: refusal rate and defect rate, derived from LLM responses to harmful prompts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Justify the resulting RSI rankings by providing empirical evidence of each LLM’s success or failure in maintaining effective guardrails across multiple threat categories.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="II LLM’s Background and Preliminaries ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> provide background and mechanics of LLMs, and highlight security and safety landscape. Section <a href="#S3" title="III LLM Deployments: Architectural Overview and Exploited Vulnerabilities ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> discuss real world LLM deployments, as well as exploitation pathways of widely used and dominant LLMs. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Evaluation Methodology ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> provides details regarding methodology of this study, highlight categories, prompt design, and evaluation metrics. Section <a href="#S5" title="V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> discusses our empirical evaluation findings and comparative analysis of the responses of all nine tested LLMs, where as Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Evaluation Results and Harm Potential ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> calculates the risk profile of LLMs using the RSI. Section <a href="#S7" title="VII Conclusion ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> summarizes and concludes this study.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM’s Background and Preliminaries</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several key milestones underscore the rapid development in LLM capabilities over recent years. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ I-A Our Findings and Key Contributions ‣ I Introduction ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the Generative Pre-trained Transformer (GPT) series from OpenAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, starting in 2018, demonstrated the effectiveness of large-scale pre-training on diverse text corpora followed by fine-tuning. The well-known ChatGPT chatbot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, for instance, is fundamentally built on OpenAI’s GPT models, leveraging deep learning and a transformer-based design to generate human-like responses. During the same time, Google’s Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, released in 2018, revolutionized NLP with its bidirectional pre-training approach, capturing contextual information from both left and right contexts, leading to state-of-the-art performance in different NLP tasks. Subsequent models like XLNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> and T5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> further refined pre-training objectives and unified NLP tasks, contributing to the versatility of LLMs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Large Language Models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models represent a significant advancement in artificial intelligence, demonstrating remarkable capabilities across diverse natural language processing (NLP) tasks. Their evolution from rudimentary systems to sophisticated neural networks has reshaped human-computer interaction and information processing. This section provides a foundational understanding of LLMs, tracing their historical development, architectural innovations, and key training methodologies, thereby establishing the context for a detailed examination of their security and safety implications as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A1 Evolution and Foundational Architectures ‣ II-A Large Language Models ‣ II LLM’s Background and Preliminaries ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span>1 </span>Evolution and Foundational Architectures</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The trajectory of LLMs can be traced from early rule-based systems, which struggled with the complexity of natural language, to the emergence of statistical methods in the late twentieth century, such as N-gram models. A pivotal shift occurred with breakthroughs in deep learning and neural networks in the early 2010s. Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks marked a new era by enabling the capture of complex linguistic patterns and contextual dependencies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most significant architectural innovation, however, was the advent of the Transformer architecture, introduced in the seminal paper ”Attention Is All You Need” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. This architecture revolutionized the field by employing a self-attention mechanism that allows models to process input sequences in parallel, overcoming the sequential processing limitations of RNNs and LSTMs. This innovation became the standard backbone for modern LLMs, enabling the development of models with billions of parameters. Subsequent models like Google’s Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and OpenAI’s Generative Pre-trained Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> series further refined pre-training and fine-tuning methodologies, solidifying the transformer’s role as the foundation for state-of-the-art LLMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10655v1/LLMLifecycle-1.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/169;" width="476" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Illustration of the Large Language Model (LLM) lifecycle, covering pre-training on large-scale corpora, instruction tuning for task adaptability, and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) for alignment, followed by deployment and continuous monitoring to ensure safety and efficiency.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span>2 </span>The Mechanics of an LLM: From Text to Prediction</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The architecture of a modern LLMs is defined by its ability to convert human language into a format it can process and then predict the most probable next word or token. This is a multi-step process that occurs within its deep neural network. Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A1 Evolution and Foundational Architectures ‣ II-A Large Language Models ‣ II LLM’s Background and Preliminaries ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the schematic of the internal architecture, and a high-level flow from tokenization through training and deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The process begins with <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tokenization</span>, where input text is broken down into smaller, manageable units called tokens. A token can be a full word, a subword, or a punctuation mark. For example, the word ”empowers” might be split into two tokens. This tokenized text is then converted into a numerical format through embeddings, which are multi-dimensional vectors that capture the semantic meaning and contextual relationships of each token. An embedding for the word ”king” might be numerically similar to the embedding for ”ruler,” a concept that traditional machine learning models struggled to grasp. These embeddings, combined with positional encoding that provides information about the token’s location in the sequence, form the final numerical representation of the input text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The core of the model’s processing lies in the <span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Transformer Block</span>. This block contains two main components: a multi-head self-attention mechanism and a Multilayer Perceptron (MLP). The attention mechanism is the key innovation; it allows tokens to ”communicate” with each other and assign varying degrees of importance to other tokens in the sequence. This enables the model to build an intricate understanding of the entire input, regardless of the distance between words. For example, in a long sentence, the model can relate a pronoun to its correct noun, a task that was difficult for earlier architectures. The MLP layer then refines the representation of each token independently. These blocks are stacked sequentially, allowing the model to build an increasingly sophisticated understanding of the input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the processed embeddings are transformed into a probability distribution for the model’s entire vocabulary. The model then predicts the next token in the sequence by sampling from this distribution. The inherent probabilistic and self-learning nature of this process is the very foundation of both a model’s remarkable capabilities and its susceptibility to novel security vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span>3 </span>The LLM Training Lifecycle: Building Capability and Alignment</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The impressive performance of modern LLMs is a result of a <span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multi-phased</span> training process as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-A1 Evolution and Foundational Architectures ‣ II-A Large Language Models ‣ II LLM’s Background and Preliminaries ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
The <span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">first</span> phase is self-supervised learning, or pre-training. During this stage, the model is exposed to a massive, unannotated corpus of text from the internet and other sources like Common Crawl and Wikipedia. It learns to predict ”missing” pieces of this data, enabling it to acquire a foundational understanding of grammar, language patterns, and general knowledge.
The <span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">second</span> phase is supervised learning, or instruction tuning. The model is fine-tuned on a smaller, curated dataset of examples that demonstrate how to follow specific instructions. This phase teaches the model to be more interactive and useful, moving beyond simple next-token prediction to responding to user requests.
The <span id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">final</span> and most critical phase for safety is Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF). In this stage, the model’s outputs are graded by human annotators. A reward model is trained on these human preferences, and the LLM is then optimized to maximize the reward signal. This is instrumental in aligning the model with desired safety guidelines, reducing the generation of harmful, biased, or inappropriate content. The effectiveness of this process directly determines a model’s ability to resist adversarial inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, models like DeepSeek V3 and R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> have introduced advanced architectural innovations such as the Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) and Multi-Head Latent Attention (MLA). The MoE architecture, for example, replaces traditional feed-forward neural networks with a group of smaller, specialized expert networks. This design allows for the activation of only a subset of the model’s total parameters per input token, significantly lowering inference costs while maintaining high performance. DeepSeek’s implementation of MoE further refines this by incorporating fine-grained expert segmentation and shared expert isolation, which reduces redundancy among experts and enhances parameter efficiency. Similarly, MLA extends Multi-Head Attention by compressing key-value pairs into low-dimensional latent representations, thereby decreasing cache size, reducing memory usage, and accelerating inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The progression from foundational Transformer models to specialized architectural enhancements like MoE and MLA reflects a broader trend in LLM development. Although the Transformer architecture established a robust base for processing long-range dependencies, subsequent innovations are not merely about increasing model size but rather about developing smarter, more efficient architectures. This emphasis on efficiency, particularly evident in DeepSeek having an impressive performance-to-price ratio, directly addresses the significant computational and energy costs associated with deploying and maintaining large-scale LLMs. As these models become increasingly ubiquitous across industries, the economic and environmental pressures to optimize their operational footprint will likely drive continued research into new hybrid architectures that balance capability with efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Defining characteristics of LLMs include their composition as deep neural networks, often with billions of parameters, trained through self-supervised learning on massive unsupervised datasets. In this process, the models learn intricate knowledge, linguistic rules, and complex patterns embedded within the corpus, enabling them to excel in diverse tasks such as machine translation, text summarization, logical reasoning, and question-answering dialogues, often surpassing traditional deep neural network models. Their underlying technologies leverage deep learning techniques, particularly neural networks, to analyze language patterns, and their sheer scale allows them to capture complex linguistic structures and nuances.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Security and Safety Landscape</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of LLMs into critical systems across various industries has brought to the forefront a complex array of security threats and safety concerns. These challenges span the entire life-cycle of LLMs, from their initial training phases to their deployment and ongoing operation. Addressing these vulnerabilities is paramount to ensuring the reliability, trustworthiness, and ethical operation of AI systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>Taxonomy of Risks in LLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The widespread integration of large language models (LLMs) across sectors calls for a clear, practical view of both the threats they face and the frameworks we use to judge those risks. In this work, we adopt a unified taxonomy that groups challenges into three pillars—<span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">security</span>, <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">safety</span>, and <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">privacy</span>—with <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ethical considerations</span> permeating all three. We then outline adversarial capabilities and real-world scenarios that stress these pillars in practice. Community guidance such as the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications primarily addresses the <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">security</span> surface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>; by contrast, widely adopted, practice-ready frameworks for <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">safety</span> and <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">privacy</span> are still maturing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security</span> in LLMs primarily addresses the model’s vulnerability to intentional attacks by adversaries. It focuses on the model’s resilience against manipulations that could result in incorrect, misleading, or malicious outputs. This category of risks aims to prevent unauthorized access, modification, malfunctioning, or denial of service to authorized users during normal operation. Common security threats include:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backdoor attacks</span>: This attack involves the malicious insertion of hidden triggers or corrupted data during the training process to cause the model to produce harmful or targeted outputs when specific inputs are provided.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Injection:</span> This is a concerning vulnerability where attackers manipulate inputs to force the AI to disregard its original instructions and generate unintended or harmful outputs. This can lead to the exposure of sensitive data, the execution of unauthorized actions, or the generation of malicious content. Techniques include disguising malicious instructions as benign inputs or ”jailbreaking” the LLM to bypass its inherent safeguards.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Poisoning:</span> Threat actors can inject manipulated or malicious data during the pre-training or fine-tuning phases of an LLM. This insidious attack corrupts the model’s behavior, subtly influencing outputs, introducing hidden backdoors, or creating biases that may remain undetected during regular validation processes. Such actions compromise the integrity of the training data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitive Information Disclosure a.k.a Privacy Leakage:</span> LLMs can inadvertently generate outputs containing sensitive information that was learned from their training data or from prior user interactions, thereby compromising confidentiality. This includes privacy leakage resulting from the exploitation of publicly available data, the misuse of personal input provided by users, and unauthorized access to the model’s internal data or outputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adversarial Attacks:</span> These involve crafting inputs that appear harmless to human reviewers but are specifically designed to confuse the model and trigger unintended responses by exploiting weaknesses in the AI’s decision logic. A notable subset is <span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adversarial Hallucination Attacks</span>, where fabricated details embedded within prompts lead the model to produce or elaborate on false information, posing significant risks, particularly in high-stakes domains like clinical decision support.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Inversion and Data Reconstruction:</span> Adversaries attempt to reconstruct sensitive information from the AI’s training data by systematically analyzing its outputs, even without direct access to the stored data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unauthorized Access to Accounts:</span> Lack of strict authentication and access controls can allow attackers to gain unauthorized access to user accounts and their stored chat histories, leading to the extraction of sensitive information or impersonation within critical systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Manipulation of Model Outputs:</span> This involves intentionally influencing LLM outputs through repeated biased interactions or by manipulating fine-tuning datasets, resulting in skewed responses that promote disinformation or harmful content.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Denial of Service Attacks:</span> Attackers can overwhelm the LLM system with a high volume of complex or resource-intensive queries, degrading its performance or rendering the service unavailable to legitimate users.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.I1.i10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insecure Plugin Designs</span>, which can create pathways for unauthorized system access. The sources also broadly classify cybersecurity violations as encompassing malware engineering, social engineering attacks, and generating insecure code.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety</span> refers to the model’s inherent reliability and robustness in the absence of external threats. It is concerned with the model’s ability to function correctly and consistently without unintended behaviors or errors, ensuring adherence to ethical guidelines and avoidance of harmful outputs. Key safety concerns identified in LLMs include:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bias and Fairness:</span>
Bias in LLMs emerges when training corpora encode historical inequities or reflect skewed distributions of social, racial, or gender representations. These biases can manifest in downstream predictions or classifications, resulting in unfair treatment of certain groups. A seminal investigation by Buolamwini and Gebru demonstrated that commercial facial recognition systems exhibited stark disparities, with error rates as high as 34% for dark-skinned women compared to less than 1% for light-skinned men <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. This finding highlighted how under-representation in training datasets can lead to systematic harms.
Similar patterns emerge in decision-making applications. Recent work has shown that AI models trained on historical mortgage data reproduced discriminatory lending patterns, with loan denial rates disproportionately affecting applicants of color despite equivalent financial profiles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. These findings underscore the risks of perpetuating structural inequalities when LLMs are deployed in socially sensitive domains without bias mitigation strategies. Beyond financial services, biased LLM outputs can influence hiring recommendations, healthcare triage, and even predictive policing—contexts where fairness is paramount.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucination:</span>
Hallucination refers to the generation of plausible but factually inaccurate or fabricated content by LLMs. Unlike traditional classification errors, hallucinations are particularly insidious because the outputs often appear syntactically correct and semantically convincing. This phenomenon has been observed in models generating fabricated citations in scientific writing or producing non-existent legal precedents when queried for case law <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Hallucinations arise due to the probabilistic next-token prediction nature of transformers, where coherence is optimized without guarantees of factual grounding.
The implications are severe in high-stakes settings. In healthcare, hallucinated treatment plans or drug interactions could lead to unsafe recommendations. In journalism and content generation, fabricated facts risk undermining public trust. Mitigation efforts range from retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) techniques that ground outputs in verified corpora to post-generation fact-checking systems. Nevertheless, hallucination remains a central obstacle in advancing the reliability of LLM-powered systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Toxicity:</span>
Toxicity in LLMs refers to the unintended production of offensive, hateful, or harmful language. Because pre-training relies on large corpora scraped from the internet, models inevitably ingest toxic discourse. Without effective filtering or alignment, this data manifests in outputs ranging from subtle microaggressions to overt hate speech <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. Such content not only undermines user trust but also risks legal and reputation consequences for deploying organizations.
Empirical studies show that toxicity rates vary depending on demographic cues in prompts: for instance, prompts referencing marginalized groups are more likely to elicit harmful completions than neutral queries. This bias mirrors broader online discourse patterns but, when amplified by LLMs, may perpetuate harm at scale. Mitigation efforts include reinforcement learning with human feedback (RLHF), toxicity classifiers for output filtering, and dataset curation techniques. Yet trade-offs remain between safety, utility, and free expression. Addressing toxicity thus requires a multidisciplinary approach, combining technical alignment strategies with societal oversight.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy and Ethics</span> in the context of LLMs involves safeguarding against adverse societal impacts and ensuring responsible AI development and deployment. This includes addressing fundamental questions regarding fairness, accountability, and the responsible use of AI systems. Ethical challenges often overlap with safety and privacy concerns, such as:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The generation of harmful or inappropriate material, like hate speech, or the perpetuation of biases.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Privacy implications, particularly regarding the inadvertent disclosure of sensitive personal information from training data or user interactions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Issues of over-reliance on AI-generated content, especially when it is biased or inaccurate.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The potential for LLMs to exhibit racism, disregard for human life, or endorsement of stereotypes, as noted in evaluations of models like DeepSeek.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Concerns related to intellectual property infringement, where LLMs might produce copyrighted material or provide access to pirated resources.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>2 </span>Novel Adversarial Attacks in LLMs</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adversarial capabilities refer to the intentional crafting of inputs or manipulation of models to trigger unintended, harmful, or malicious outputs. These capabilities are exploited in various real-world scenarios across diverse domains. Attackers often employ strategies to gain trust, make seemingly innocent requests, or avoid disclaimers to bypass guardrails.
Specific adversarial attack types in LLMs and their real-world implications include:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Hacking and Injection</span>: This involves strategically designing and manipulating input prompts to influence an LLM’s output. It can cause models to disregard previous instructions or perform actions intended by the attacker, leading to data leakage, unauthorized access, or generation of hate speech or fake news. Examples include instructing the model to ”Ignore previous prompt” or using black-box methods like HOUYI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> which leverage pre-constructed prompts and malicious payloads. Prompt injection attacks have been demonstrated on LLM-integrated applications and can even extract underlying application prompts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreaking Attacks</span>: These techniques bypass the safety mechanisms and constraints built into LLMs to generate content that would typically be prohibited. This can lead to the generation of toxic, manipulative, racist, illegal, or offensive content. Attack methods include character role-play (e.g., ”DAN- Do Anything Now”) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, nested prompts (DeepInception), or automated methods like MASTERKEY . Multi-step jailbreaking<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> has been shown to enable leakage of Personally Identifiable Information (PII) like email addresses and phone numbers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Backdoor and Poisoning Attacks</span>: These security threats compromise the LLM’s behavior by embedding hidden triggers or corrupting training data. Once activated by specific input patterns, the model exhibits unintended behaviors, such as generating misleading or malicious outputs. Examples include instruction poisoning and in-context learning (ICL) poisoning, BadEdit, BadGPT, and Trojan attacks like TROJANLM and TrojanPuzzle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. These attacks can lead to systemic errors, expose private data, or cause models to produce pre-defined malicious code.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 2: </span>Comparison of prior works on LLMs safety &amp; security evaluations highlighting key contributions, risk categories, evaluation metrics, adversarial inputs, methodologies, and evaluators. </figcaption>
