</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prior Research Work on LLM Risk Evaluation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As summarized in Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="TABLE 2 ‣ II-B2 Novel Adversarial Attacks in LLMs ‣ II-B LLM Security and Safety Landscape ‣ II LLM’s Background and Preliminaries ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the literature falls into <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">three</span> complementary strands. <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First</span> are evaluation frameworks that stress realism and scale. Jindal et al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> developed personality-aware, conversational harm evaluations across five to seven LLMs and report standard safety metrics such as <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defect</span>, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Refusal</span>, and <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safety usability Index - SUI</span> under controlled prompting regimes. Similarly, Omar et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> adapt this evaluation to clinical decision support exposing hallucination-driven risks via 300 physician-designed vignettes. Their finding illustrates how factuality and safety failures can co-occur when LLMs produce plausible but harmful outputs in high-stake domains. Together, these works provided a structured pipelines emphasizing on narrow harm categories (e.g., sexual, violence, misinformation, or medical errors) and limited model sets, making generalization difficult.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second</span> is the taxonomy and survey papers that map the threat surface and defenses. Abdali et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> provides an end to end threat taxonomy (SecLM) that spans the full life cycle of LLM systems and investigates security/privacy concerns related to LLMs from five thematic perspectives: security and privacy concerns, vulnerabilities against adversarial attacks, potential harms, mitigation strategies and future research avenues. Aguilera-Martínez et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> also defines and analyzes attacks on LLMs and discusses possible defense mechanism, while also evaluating the effectiveness of the known defense mechanisms. The broader surveys extend this view; the 2024 LLM Security &amp; Privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> reviews the existing progress in LLM by categorizing it into beneficial applications, offensive applications, and the vulnerabilities of LLMs. They have focused on how LLM can bolster as well as jeopardize the cybersecurity. Work by Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> also provides a comprehensive overview of these emerging concerns focusing on inference-time attacks, training-time attacks, misuse by malicious actors, and the inherent risks in autonomous LLM agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">third</span> is the empirical red-team studies and methodological critiques. Beyer et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> expose brittleness in prevailing safety evaluations by analyzing the LLM safety evaluation pipelines, and discussing optimization strategies for automated red-teaming, response generation, and evaluation using LLM judges. At all stages of the LLM safety evaluation pipeline, the study by Beyer et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> identifies the key issues with their practical impact. Pathade et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> provides systematic evaluation of jailbreak strategies against state-of-the-art LLMs, while also proposing layered mitigation strategies, with a red-teaming recommendation for robust LLM security. A PRISMA-style comprehensive literature review by Hasanov et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> covers offensive, defensive, ethical, legal, and governance perspectives on LLM applications in cybersecurity. The work provides statistical mapping of global research trends while highlighting the growing roles LLMs in security innovations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An examination of Table <a href="#S2.T2" title="TABLE 2 ‣ II-B2 Novel Adversarial Attacks in LLMs ‣ II-B LLM Security and Safety Landscape ‣ II LLM’s Background and Preliminaries ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reveals two cross-cutting patterns: some studies emphasize a limited set of risk categories evaluated across multiple LLMs, whereas others emphasize a limited set of models examined across multiple categories. These patterns motivated the design of our methodology. Metrics and realism are heterogeneous; some works emphasize model-centric rates such as defect, refusal, SUI; others report Attack Success Rate (ASR) under varied adversarial inputs (image-seeded, code-switching, or application-integrated prompts). Scale varies widely—from conceptual surveys to multi-thousand-prompt audits—complicating fair comparison. Moreover, “Policies” are inconsistently defined (often none), and evaluators range from human rubrics to LLM-as-judge, which can shift reported safety levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given these limitations and inconsistencies across related works, our empirical study across nine different LLMs adopt single-turn, transferable adversarial prompts across a broader harm palette than the classic sexual/violence/misinformation triad, and we report a modular Risk Severity Index (RSI) that jointly reflects refusal and defect behavior—providing a simple, comparable summary while remaining compatible with the taxonomies and red-team practices cited above.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">LLM Deployments: Architectural Overview and Exploited Vulnerabilities</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The deployment of LLMs has expanded rapidly across sectors, ranging from enterprise applications in finance and healthcare to public services and education. Organizations increasingly adopt LLMs to automate customer service, accelerate programming tasks, and support decision-making. However, this rapid adoption has been paralleled by a surge in adversarial exploitation strategies targeting LLMs vulnerabilities.
Alongside these opportunities, LLMs face growing risks of malicious misuse. Prompt injection and jailbreak attacks can manipulate models into overriding safety protocols, leading to the generation of disallowed or harmful content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>. A recent case study demonstrated how Google’s Gemini model could be compromised via a malicious calendar invitation—so-called “promptware”— to open smart shutters, download unauthorized files, and leak private messages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>.
Beyond jailbreaks, researchers have shown how LLMs can be misused to automate spear-phishing campaigns, such as generating targeted emails for over 600 UK Members of Parliament at minimal cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>. Empirical evaluations also indicate that LLM-based jailbreak and data-leakage attacks can execute in under 42 seconds, with success rates as high as 90% <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>. Threat actors further exploit LLM ecosystems for “SEO poisoning,” where malicious actors publish fake technical support content on trusted domains that LLM-powered assistants later amplify as legitimate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>.
Finally, LLMs have been implicated in broader socio-technical exploitation. Disinformation campaigns leverage LLMs to mass-generate persuasive propaganda, while deepfake-enabled scams have surged: in 2024 alone, CEO impersonation incidents driven by generative AI voice and video synthesis led to over 105,000 reported cases of fraud in the U.S. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These incidents illustrate the dual-use nature of LLMs. While they democratize access to powerful tools for productivity and innovation, they simultaneously lower the barrier for cyberattacks, disinformation, and large-scale social engineering. As deployment accelerates, the need for proactive defense measures, red-teaming, and regulatory frameworks becomes increasingly urgent.
In response, defensive measures and governance frameworks are emerging. Red-teaming and adversarial testing have become standard in responsible model release pipelines, enabling systematic identification of vulnerabilities prior to public deployment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>. Technical interventions such as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), toxicity filtering, and model watermarking are being deployed to mitigate risks. On the policy front, governments and international organizations are advancing AI governance efforts, such as the EU AI Act and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, which explicitly consider risks arising from LLM misuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>. These parallel trends of widespread adoption and sophisticated exploitation underscore the urgent need for robust socio-technical safeguards in LLM deployment.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Open- vs Closed-Source Model Tradeoffs</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The AI industry is marked by a critical dilemma: whether to develop and deploy models as closed-source or open-source systems. This choice has profound implications for a model’s security, safety, and operational philosophy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Closed-source models, such as OpenAI’s GPT and Google’s Gemini, offer state-of-the-art performance and are often positioned as a means of safeguarding proprietary knowledge and ensuring security through rigorous, vendor-led testing. This approach typically provides a more user-friendly interface and robust professional support. However, it comes at the cost of higher licensing fees, limited flexibility, and a lack of transparency into the model’s inner workings. Users must rely entirely on the vendor for security, and vulnerabilities are often not public until a patch is released, creating a dependency on the vendor’s response time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, open-source models, exemplified by Meta’s LLaMA and Mistral AI’s Mistral, democratize access to model architectures and training datasets, fostering a collaborative ecosystem. This transparency enables community-driven security audits, which can lead to faster discovery and patching of vulnerabilities. The open nature of these models also provides unparalleled control and customizability for businesses to fine-tune a model to their specific needs. However, the same ”white-box” access that allows for community scrutiny can be exploited by malicious actors for targeted attacks, such as prompt injection and model poisoning. The ability for anyone to fine-tune an open-source model also means that the safety and ethical profile can vary widely, potentially leading to the creation of harmful versions if misused. This highlights how this philosophical difference manifests in a model’s security posture.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Real World LLM Deployments and Exploits</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Against this backdrop, we first outline common <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deployment archetypes</em>—the loci where control planes, data flows, and permissions are defined—before mapping the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exploitation pathways</em> observed in the wild and the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">guardrail patterns</em> used in practice. The provider-specific subsections that follow apply this lens to ChatGPT/OpenAI, Gemini/Google, Claude/Anthropic, Mistral, LLaMA/Meta, DeepSeek, and Grok/xAI.
LLMs are no longer confined to research benchmarks: they are embedded in consumer products, enterprise workflows, developer tool-chains, and agentic systems that can browse, code, transact, and control devices. This shift expands the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">socio-technical</em> attack surface beyond the model prompt to include connectors, plugins, memory, retrieval, and policy layers. Provider reports on malicious use and incident response, together with security guidance on prompt injection, show that many failures emerge at the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">integration boundary</em> rather than from model parameters alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>. These observations motivate a deployment-first view of LLM security. Many incidents arise not from the model parameters, but from the identity, data-flow, and permission boundaries that wrap them. We therefore start by characterizing common deployment patterns and their control planes, then map the exploitation pathways seen in the wild and the guardrail practices used in response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following subsections start with a general discussion on real-world deployment patterns and exploitation pathways of widely used and dominant LLMs.
Then, we discuss specifics LLMs used in our study including ChatGPT/OpenAI, Gemini/Google, Claude/Anthropic, Mistral, LLaMA/Meta, DeepSeek, and Grok/xAI, and follow a common structure: <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">architecture and training</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">security and safety profile</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">real-world signals (misuse/abuse)</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">privacy &amp; ethics</span>, concluding with deployment takeaways specific to each ecosystem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deployment Patterns:</span>
We observe four common patterns in the wild: (i) hosted assistants with provider-managed guardrails (e.g., consumer chat surfaces); (ii) API-based integrations that bind models to tools (browsers, file systems, calendars, smart-home devices); (iii) self-hosted/open-weight deployments for customization and cost control; and (iv) on-device or edge variants for privacy/latency. Each pattern implies a different control plane for identity, data flow, and permissions—hence different failure modes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploitation pathways:</span> Attackers routinely combine <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">direct</em> jailbreaks with <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">indirect</em> prompt injection (poisoned web pages, emails, documents) to steer models toward unsafe actions or data exfiltration. Insecure tool bindings (missing confirmations, over-scoped permissions) and unsafe output handling compound impact. Beyond prompting, fine-tuning or system-prompt editing can erode guardrails in open-weight deployments. Critically, several national and vendor advisories treat indirect prompt injection as a <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">content-borne</em> attack that must be mitigated at the product layer (sanitization, allow/deny policies, confirmations) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy and regulation:</span> At web scale, LLMs face risks of memorization and leakage of sensitive data; enterprise usage introduces additional obligations around retention, access, and provenance. In jurisdictions such as the EU, the GDPR and the AI Act set expectations for transparency, data protection, and governance across providers and deployers, which directly shape design choices for logging, data minimization, and user rights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Challenges:</span> Safety measurements can be brittle across prompts, templates, and sampling hyperparameters, complicating cross-paper and cross-model comparisons. Recent work calls for standardized reporting and robust benchmarks (e.g., JailbreakBench) to reduce measurement noise and better reflect deployment realities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>ChatGPT (OpenAI)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OpenAI’s ChatGPT (GPT<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>3.5/4 family) popularized instruction-following LLMs paired with post-training alignment (e.g., RLHF) and safety tooling. The public product line mixes hosted assistants, APIs, and sandboxed tools (e.g., browsing/code execution), which elevates both capability and the socio-technical attack surface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architectural innovation and training.</span>
ChatGPT builds on transformer decoders scaled with large-corpus pretraining and instruction tuning, with RLHF and policy filtering to reduce harmful outputs while preserving helpfulness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security and safety profile.</span>
As a widely deployed, tool-integrated assistant, ChatGPT faces canonical LLM risks (prompt/indirect prompt injection, data leakage, bias amplification) and product risks (plugin/tool abuse, feature mis-scoping)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>. OpenAI publishes periodic disruption reports on malicious use, and has temporarily constrained features when safety controls fell short <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world signals (misuse/abuse).</span> Beyond synthetic benchmarks, multiple public incidents illustrate practical risk surfaces:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Payment/metadata exposure via third-party bug (2023).</em> A Redis library bug led to transient exposure of some chat titles and limited billing data for a subset of Plus users; OpenAI disabled ChatGPT, remediated, and notified affected users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>, <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Enterprise data leakage (Samsung case).</em> Employees pasted sensitive source code into ChatGPT, prompting a corporate ban on generative-AI tools; this highlights organizational, not model, controls as the primary safeguard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Internal forum breach disclosure (2023 event, reported 2024).</em> An external actor accessed an employee discussion forum; OpenAI reported no customer data/system access but hardened processes thereafter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>, <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Malicious-use telemetry (state/criminal actors).</em> OpenAI’s threat-intel updates document attempts to use its models for parts of intrusion workflows (e.g., recon scripting, translation, influence content), followed by account takedowns and policy enforcement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Direct prompt-injection data exfiltration.</em> Researchers showed that GPT<span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>4/4o could be induced to disclose personal data via crafted prompts (exacerbated by memory features), illustrating pure prompt-surface risk even without plugins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Indirect prompt injection (plugins/browsing).</em> National guidance warned that chatbots with web/mail/code plugins can be steered by poisoned content (links, docs) to exfiltrate secrets or perform unintended actions; these concerns informed hardened browsing/tooling practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>. In a separate policy response, OpenAI briefly disabled “Browse with Bing” in 2023 after it surfaced paywalled text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">7.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Offensive capability lowering the barrier to entry.</em> Security researchers and law-enforcement analyses observed early cases of malware scaffolding and phishing text generated with ChatGPT, underscoring the need for abuse detection and usage controls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy and ethics.</span>
At web-scale, memorization/leakage and downstream bias remain salient. In practice, enterprise deployments gate ChatGPT with data-handling controls (DLP, logging, red-teaming) and defense-in-depth around plugins/tool use, alongside policy/monitoring informed by provider telemetry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>DeepDive: DeepSeek (DeepSeek AI)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DeepSeek, developed by a Chinese firm, has emerged as a significant player in the LLM landscape, particularly noted for its efficiency and performance. However, its innovations are accompanied by distinct security vulnerabilities and ethical considerations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Architectural Innovation and Training Methodology:</span>
DeepSeek V3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and R1 models represent substantial advancements in LLM architecture, primarily focusing on achieving an impressive performance-to-price ratio. DeepSeek V3 builds upon the Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture, activating only a subset of its vast parameter count (e.g., <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">37 billion out of 671 billion total parameters</span>) per token, significantly lowering inference costs. DeepSeek V2 and V3 introduce Multi-Head Latent Attention (MLA), which compresses key-value pairs to reduce memory usage and accelerate inference.
In terms of training, DeepSeek V3 utilizes mixed-precision training (FP8). The R1 models notably focused on reinforcement learning, including DeepSeek R1-Zero (RL without human feedback) and DeepSeek R1 (multi-step process including SFT, GPRO, and additional RL).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Identified Security Vulnerabilities and Lessons from Breaches:</span>
DeepSeek has been found to possess serious security vulnerabilities, including the potential to leak sensitive personal information and a notable susceptibility to malicious prompt-based attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exposed database and key leakage.</span> Wiz researchers found a publicly accessible DeepSeek ClickHouse instance with over a million log lines, chat histories, API secrets, and backend details. Beyond immediate confidentiality loss, this kind of exposure enables replay of session tokens, scraping of prompt/response corpora for targeted jailbreaks, service impersonation via leaked keys, and mapping of internal endpoints.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak susceptibility at scale.</span> Qualys evaluated 885 attacks across 18 jailbreak types and observed a 58% failure rate, indicating frequent guardrail bypass under standardized adversarial prompts. In practice, both transferable single-turn prompts and multi-turn strategies (e.g., “Crescendo”) degrade policy adherence, with safety filters regressing under conversational accommodation. The takeaway is to assume jailbreaks will occur in the wild and to layer model-level alignment with product-layer controls (e.g., I/O filtering, confirmation gates, rate/permission limits) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">93</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High harmful-output rates in red teaming.</span> Enkrypt AI’s testing reported DeepSeek<span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>R1 as markedly more likely to produce hazardous or toxic content than some competitors across sensitive categories (e.g., CBRN, self-harm, sexual content). While external and not peer-reviewed, the findings motivated practitioner guidance on pre-deployment safety gates, continuous red teaming, and post-deployment monitoring for abuse signals; Cloud Security Alliance summarized these mitigations for security teams <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broader risk assessments.</span> Practitioner analyses emphasize that risk is socio-technical: prompt injection chained to tool invocation, insecure output handling in agents, supply-chain exposure for model artifacts, and weak tenant isolation. Recommended mitigations include isolation of inference tiers, strict egress controls, retrieval grounding with allow/deny policies, least-privilege tool bindings, and continuous abuse telemetry—framed as part of a broader governance program <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The DeepSeek AI breach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite>, which exposed over a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">million critical records</span>, underscored the importance of robust cybersecurity measures for LLMs. This incident highlighted that traditional security methods are insufficient, necessitating specialized penetration testing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Regulatory and Ethical Implications:</span>
The release of DeepSeek V3 and R1 by a Chinese firm has raised considerable concerns about potential ”strategic surprise” and increased exposure to AI risks globally.
The release and rapid adoption of DeepSeek V3 and R1 by a Chinese provider has prompted concern over potential “strategic surprise” and elevated systemic risk exposure across jurisdictions. Below we summarize salient policy and ethics signals using public sources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data privacy.</span> Analyses warn that use of the DeepSeek web API may entail cross-border transfers and retention under PRC jurisdiction, raising confidentiality and oversight concerns for certain sectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regulatory responses.</span> Following disclosures and heightened scrutiny, several governments and regulators issued guidance or took action consistent with their AI and government-IT policies:</p>
<ul id="S3.I3.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">United States.</span> U.S. Commerce Department bureaus restricted DeepSeek on government devices; congressional oversight flagged risks for federal use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">European Union.</span> Under the EU AI Act, <em id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">general-purpose AI (GPAI)</em> and <em id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPAI models with systemic risk</em> carry defined obligations—clarified in the Commission’s Q&amp;A; notably, there is no blanket “high-risk” label for all LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Japan.</span> METI launched the AI Safety Institute (J - AISI) to strengthen national AI safety governance and evaluation (Feb. 2024) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">South Korea.</span> The Personal Information Protection Commission (PIPC) announced suspension of DeepSeek’s service in Korea effective Feb. 15, 2025 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ethical performance.</span> Using the Relative Danger Coefficient (RDC), comparative audits report “large problems in ethical terms,” including stereotype endorsement and diminished regard for human life in edge cases; separate analyses observe <em id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pro-Chinese political bias</em> in DeepSeek R1 across tasks and languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>, <a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">102</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>3 </span>Claude (Anthropic)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Anthropic’s Claude family emphasizes safety-first model design and transparency (system/model cards, responsible scaling), alongside iterative defenses against jailbreak and prompt-injection attacks. Recent releases (e.g., <em id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Claude 3/3.5</em> and <em id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">3.7 Sonnet</em>) pair inference capabilities with layered safeguards described in public cards and developer guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>, <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>, <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architectural innovation and training.</span>
Claude models are transformer decoders trained at scale and aligned via instruction tuning and policy guidance (Constitutional AI). Anthropic has introduced <em id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Constitutional Classifiers</em> to harden inputs/outputs against universal jailbreaks, and publishes system/model cards detailing evaluation scope (harms, tool use, and prompt-injection risks) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">106</a>, <a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security and safety profile.</span>
Anthropic publicly researches jailbreak vectors and releases mitigations (e.g., many-shot defenses, universal-jailbreak monitors), while cautioning that product integrations (computer use, browsing, agents) expand the attack surface and require product-layer controls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">108</a>, <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>, <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world signals (misuse/abuse).</span> Beyond synthetic benchmarks, public reports and Anthropic’s own security updates surface practical risks:</p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Large-scale cybercrime misuse (TI report).</em> Anthropic’s Threat Intelligence report (Aug 2025) details attempts to weaponize Claude for extortion campaigns, ransomware development/sales, and DPRK-linked employment fraud; implicated accounts were banned and safeguards tightened <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>, <a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Browser extension prompt injection.</em> In red-team tests for <em id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Claude for Chrome</em>, Anthropic measured a 23.6% attack success rate <em id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> mitigations across 29 attack scenarios (123 test cases)—prompting confirmations, permission scopes, and other defenses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">111</a>, <a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">112</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Many-shot jailbreaks.</em> Anthropic’s own research showed that long-context “many-shot” prompting could bypass refusals (across Claude and other vendors) and published mitigations thereafter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">108</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Multi-turn Crescendo jailbreaks.</em> Independent studies (USENIX Security ’25) demonstrate state-of-the-art multi-turn jailbreaks that escalate across turns and transfer across models, including Claude variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">113</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Automated multi-turn jailbreaks.</em> Recent work reports <math id="S3.I4.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>70% success on multi-turn attacks against frontier models (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini) using StrongREJECT-style setups—underscoring the gap between single-turn and agentic settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">114</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Universal-jailbreak hardening &amp; bug bounties.</em> Anthropic deployed <em id="S3.I4.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Constitutional Classifiers</em>, ran thousands of hours of human red teaming, and launched bounty programs for universal jailbreaks tied to high-risk content (e.g., CBRN) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">106</a>, <a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy and ethics.</span>
Claude system cards discuss risks from computer use and tool integrations (prompt injection, insecure output handling) and outline mitigations (monitoring, confirmations, least-privilege tool bindings). In practice, deployers combine model-level alignment with product controls and continuous abuse telemetry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>, <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>4 </span>Gemini (Google)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Google’s Gemini family comprises natively multimodal models (e.g., <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ultra</em>, <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pro</em>, <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Nano/Flash</em>) deployed across the Gemini app, Pixel, and Workspace. The technical report emphasizes end-to-end multimodality (text–image–audio–video) and variants balancing capability vs. latency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architectural innovation and training.</span>
Gemini trains for native multimodality rather than bolt-on adapters, with tiers for server and on-device use and broad benchmark coverage across perception, reasoning, and tool use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security and safety profile.</span>
Google describes a layered defense posture for Gemini against prompt/indirect-prompt injection—model hardening (Gemini 2.5), ML detectors for malicious instructions, output filtering, and product-level confirmations for sensitive actions—aligned with its Secure AI Framework (SAIF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world signals (misuse/abuse).</span> Beyond synthetic benchmarks, several public incidents illustrate practical risk surfaces:</p>
<ol id="S3.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Indirect prompt injection via calendar invites.</em> The “Invitation Is All You Need” campaign shows that poisoned Google Calendar entries or document titles can drive Gemini to control smart-home devices, initiate calls, leak email snippets, and more across 14 attack variants; Google added confirmations and other mitigations after disclosure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">117</a>, <a href="#bib.bib118" title="" class="ltx_ref">118</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Image-borne prompt injection (“image scaling” attacks).</em> Trail of Bits demonstrated hidden prompts embedded in images that materialize only after downscaling; Gemini CLI, Vertex AI Studio, and Assistant variants processed these images and executed unintended tool-linked behaviors. Trail of Bits released the <em id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Anamorpher</em> tool to reproduce and test these cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib119" title="" class="ltx_ref">119</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Email summarization prompt injection.</em> Gemini for Workspace could be induced to display attacker-crafted phishing text hidden inside emails when users clicked “Summarize,” prompting guidance and fixes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">120</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gemini CLI agent risk.</em> A prompt-injection flaw in the new Gemini CLI (for code/agent workflows) allowed conditions for silent execution of malicious commands or sensitive-data exfiltration from developer environments prior to patching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib121" title="" class="ltx_ref">121</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Separately, Google paused <em id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">people</em> image generation in early 2024 after integrity issues with historical depictions—an important safety failure (not a security breach) that underscores the need for product-layer guardrails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib122" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy and ethics.</span>
As a multimodal, product-integrated system, Gemini inherits memorization/leakage risks and system-level risks where outputs drive tools. In practice, deployers pair model-level alignment with product controls—content filters, retrieval grounding, permissioning/rate limits, and attack-aware evaluation—consistent with Google’s layered-defense guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>5 </span>Grok (xAI)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">xAI’s Grok family targets fast, agentic assistants integrated with the X platform and API surfaces. Public documentation describes an autoregressive transformer lineage fine-tuned with human and model feedback, with releases spanning base/open weights and hosted production tiers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite>. xAI also open-released the <em id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Grok<span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>1</em> base weights and architecture (a large MoE), enabling community replication and analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib124" title="" class="ltx_ref">124</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architectural innovation and training.</span>
Grok models scale standard decoder-only transformers with long context and tool-use hooks (function calling/structured outputs) aimed at agentic use cases, from conversational search to code and automation. The open-weight <em id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Grok<span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>1</em> release documents the Mixture-of-Experts backbone and training setup; hosted successors add larger context and multimodality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib124" title="" class="ltx_ref">124</a>, <a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security and safety profile.</span>
As a tool-integrated assistant with public and API front ends, Grok inherits canonical LLM risks (prompt/indirect prompt injection, jailbreaks, insecure output handling) plus product-layer risks (unsafe tool bindings, sharing features, indexing). Independent audits and incident reports highlight areas where guardrails erode under adversarial pressure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world signals (misuse/abuse).</span> Beyond synthetic benchmarks, public incidents and audits illustrate practical risk surfaces:</p>
<ol id="S3.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mass exposure of user chats.</em> Hundreds of thousands of Grok conversations became publicly searchable via shared links, surfacing sensitive and unsafe content; the episode underscores the need for private-by-default sharing and “noindex” controls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib125" title="" class="ltx_ref">125</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Regulatory privacy probe (EU).</em> Ireland’s DPC opened a GDPR investigation into X’s use of European users’ public posts to train Grok, spotlighting training-data governance and legal bases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib126" title="" class="ltx_ref">126</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Low jailbreak resistance (Grok-3).</em> A third-party red-team audit reported very low resistance to jailbreak prompts, recommending continuous monitoring and stronger filtering in enterprise deployments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib127" title="" class="ltx_ref">127</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Prompt injection &amp; excessive agency (Grok-4).</em> A security report documented prompt-injection susceptibility, repetitive divergence, and unauthorized-action risks (“excessive agency”) under test harnesses aligned with CyberSecEval prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib128" title="" class="ltx_ref">128</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Open-weights attack surface.</em> The open release of <em id="S3.I6.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Grok<span id="S3.I6.i5.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>1</em> increases transparency and reproducibility—but also lowers the barrier for white-box analysis, template fingerprinting, and fine-tuning that can erode safeguards if misused <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib124" title="" class="ltx_ref">124</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Search-indexed harmful instructions.</em> The chat exposure incident amplified the distribution of adversarial content (e.g., illicit instructions) via search engines, illustrating how product UX can magnify model-level risks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib125" title="" class="ltx_ref">125</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS6.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>6 </span>LLaMA (Meta)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Meta’s LLaMA family is a widely used line of open(-weight) foundation models that couples strong downstream performance with an active ecosystem of tooling and safety guidance. Recent releases (e.g., <em id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Llama 4</em>) ship alongside moderation components such as <em id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Llama Guard 2</em> and a Responsible Use Guide intended to support layered, system-level safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib129" title="" class="ltx_ref">129</a>, <a href="#bib.bib130" title="" class="ltx_ref">130</a>, <a href="#bib.bib131" title="" class="ltx_ref">131</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architectural innovation and training.</span>
LLaMA models are standard transformer decoders, scaled across sizes and instruction-tuned for helpfulness/safety. The <em id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Llama 3,4</em> family increased pretraining scale and introduced updated trust-and-safety tools (e.g., Llama Guard 2; CyberSecEval 2) to accompany open releases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib129" title="" class="ltx_ref">129</a>]</cite>. Documentation distinguishes model cards, prompt formats, and moderation adapters to encourage reproducible evaluation and deployment hygiene <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib130" title="" class="ltx_ref">130</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security and safety profile.</span>
Open weights enable rapid community scrutiny and research, but they also reduce the friction for adversarial analysis, jailbreak transfer, or post-release fine-tuning that can erode guardrails if used incautiously. Empirical work consistently finds high jailbreak Attack Success Rates (ASR) on open-weight baselines relative to some hosted systems, and proposes standardized robustness measurement (e.g., JailbreakBench) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib132" title="" class="ltx_ref">132</a>]</cite>. Concretely, researchers have shown that modest additional training can <em id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">remove</em> safety behaviors from <em id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Llama 2-Chat</em> at low cost, underscoring deployer responsibility for policy layers around open models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world signals (misuse/abuse).</span> Beyond synthetic benchmarks, several public incidents illustrate practical risk surfaces:</p>
<ol id="S3.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Unsafe self-hosted deployments (llama.cpp).</em> Misconfigured llama.cpp servers publicly exposed user prompts, including explicit role-play scenarios, underscoring how open-weight deployments can leak sensitive content if not properly secured <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib133" title="" class="ltx_ref">133</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Low-cost removal of safety tuning.</em> “BadLlama” shows that safety behaviors in <em id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Llama 2-Chat 13B</em> can be largely undone with less than 200 dollars of additional training while retaining general capability, highlighting fine-tuning as a misuse vector <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">High ASR under standardized jailbreak evaluation.</em> Benchmark studies report elevated attack-success rates on open-weight baselines (including LLaMA variants) under transferable jailbreak prompts, motivating defense-in-depth beyond model tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I7.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Information-operations tooling.</em> Threat-intel reporting and incident records describe a state-linked surveillance/propaganda system (“Peer Review”) allegedly built on LLaMA derivatives—illustrating operational misuse risks when powerful open weights are integrated into gray-box stacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib134" title="" class="ltx_ref">134</a>, <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy and ethics.</span>
As with other web-scale LMs, risks include memorization and leakage of sensitive data as well as downstream bias amplification. Meta’s guidance recommends end-to-end safeguards—data-handling controls, content filters, evaluation with realistic threat models, and incident response—implemented at the product boundary in addition to model-level tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib131" title="" class="ltx_ref">131</a>, <a href="#bib.bib130" title="" class="ltx_ref">130</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10655v1/MethodologyFlow.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/104;" width="476" height="104"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Evaluation Methodology for LLM Safety Assessment</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS7.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>7 </span>Mistral (Mistral AI)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mistral AI is a French lab known for compact, efficient models with strong downstream performance and frequent open-weight releases. The family spans fully open models (e.g., <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mistral 7B</em>, <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mixtral 8<math id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>7B</em>) and hosted “premier” models (e.g., <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mistral Large</em>) exposed via La Plateforme and cloud partners <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib135" title="" class="ltx_ref">135</a>, <a href="#bib.bib136" title="" class="ltx_ref">136</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architectural innovation and training.</span> <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mistral 7B</em> combines grouped-query attention (GQA) with sliding-window attention (SWA) to improve throughput and long-context handling at small scale. <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mixtral 8<math id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>7B</em> adopts a sparse mixture-of-experts (SMoE) design that routes each token to two experts per layer, yielding favorable cost/quality trade-offs. Both were released with open weights under Apache-2.0; <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mistral Large</em> targets higher reasoning capability as a hosted offering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib137" title="" class="ltx_ref">137</a>, <a href="#bib.bib135" title="" class="ltx_ref">135</a>]</cite>. The official model catalog distinguishes <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">open</em> vs. <em id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">premier</em> (commercial) lines, clarifying licensing and deployment paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib136" title="" class="ltx_ref">136</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Security and safety profile.</span> As with other open-weight families, transparency enables community scrutiny and rapid iteration, but also lowers barriers for adversarial analysis, jailbreaking, and downstream fine-tuning that may weaken guardrails if deployed incautiously. In practice, integrators layer instruction tuning, RLHF, and policy filters; however, deployer responsibility increases when models run outside a provider’s hosted stack. Third-party evaluations continue to probe Mistral models alongside peers, with recent agentic/multi-turn jailbreak studies reporting high attack success rates across multiple LLMs (including open-weight baselines), motivating more rigorous, system-level testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib138" title="" class="ltx_ref">138</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world signals (misuse/abuse).</span> Beyond controlled evaluations, public incidents highlight where guardrails erode under adversarial pressure and routine use:</p>
<ol id="S3.I8" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GDPR complaint and privacy-policy changes.</em> A complaint concerning <em id="S3.I8.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Le Chat</em> was filed with the French DPA (CNIL); subsequent reporting noted updates to portions of Mistral’s privacy policy, underscoring the importance of data-governance controls around chat surfaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib139" title="" class="ltx_ref">139</a>, <a href="#bib.bib140" title="" class="ltx_ref">140</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I8.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Elevated unsafe-content rates under red teaming.</em> A third-party multimodal safety report—and follow-up press coverage—alleged that selected Mistral models produced unsafe content at higher rates when subjected to adversarial prompts; while not peer-reviewed, the findings prompted calls for stronger filtering and replication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib141" title="" class="ltx_ref">141</a>, <a href="#bib.bib142" title="" class="ltx_ref">142</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Misinformation on election procedures.</em> The AI Incident Database documents inaccurate guidance on election processes (Spanish queries) by multiple systems, explicitly listing <em id="S3.I8.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mixtral 8<math id="S3.I8.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>7B</em> among implicated entities—an integrity failure salient to real deployments, even if not a privacy breach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib143" title="" class="ltx_ref">143</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Privacy, governance, and EU context.</span> As a European provider, Mistral operates under the GDPR and the EU AI Act as they phase in, shaping expectations for data protection, transparency, and accountability across providers and deployers. Company materials document user-rights handling (GDPR requests), a DPA for processor relationships, and terms that require customers to meet legal obligations and execute the DPA where personal data are processed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib144" title="" class="ltx_ref">144</a>, <a href="#bib.bib145" title="" class="ltx_ref">145</a>]</cite>. In sum, open-weight availability accelerates research and customization, but shifts more of the safety and compliance burden to integrators; recent incident reports and red-team findings underscore the need for robust guardrailing, privacy governance, and task-specific evaluations in real applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Evaluation Methodology</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our risk evaluation process as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B6 LLaMA (Meta) ‣ III-B Real World LLM Deployments and Exploits ‣ III LLM Deployments: Architectural Overview and Exploited Vulnerabilities ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> began with outlining the major harm categories to be used in the assessment of LLMs. The harm category design process highlighted well-known harms that could result from LLM misuse, and was expanded to include less widely-known harms and more narrowly focused categories for specific topics of interest. After all of the categories were created, they were sorted into one of five broader harm topics for better organization. Once the harm categories were clearly defined, our team designed prompts by testing them on the DeepSeek V3 model and selecting those that were able to bypass its ethical guardrails, with the goal of identifying definitively harmful prompts that could be compared across other LLMs to benchmark their safety. Prompts were designed with a wider variance of topics for categories that encompassed broader harms. More specific categories, on the other hand, saw prompts with more tailored and specific aims relating to the purpose of the category. Prompts were refined to remove most of the information that was irrelevant to showcasing the harm of the response and then tested on each of nine candidate LLMs. Once responses were gathered from LLMs, our team of experts manually classified each response according to the annotation guidelines. Each classification was subject to a double-check by multiple experts to ensure correctness. The evaluation of LLM responses is conducted using three <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">key metrics</span>: the <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defect Rate</span> (the percentage of occurrences where LLM’s response exceeds harm threshold), the <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Refusal Rate</span> (the percentage of occurrences where LLMs refuses to interact), and the derived <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Risk Severity Index (RSI)</span>. The derived RSI is used to quantify harm potential. Each model is assessed by aggregating these metrics across broader harm categories as well as computing the overall average across all harm topics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Identified Harm Categories and Thresholds</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our evaluation of risks associated with certain LLMs focuses on the performance of LLM content filtering systems across 24 categories divided into 17 safety and 7 security topics as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE 3 ‣ IV-A Identified Harm Categories and Thresholds ‣ IV Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Evaluation Methodology ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Security harm categories cover responses that can lead to threats to cybersecurity, and safety harms cover responses that could harm individuals, entities, or society. Next, we outline each category and provide an associated harm <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">threshold</span>. Later in the evaluation process, we use this threshold to classify whether each LLM’s response to a prompt is <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actually</span> harmful. Each category is encompassed under one of seven harm topics as discussed below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
