<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Across the responses in the prompt referring to violent crimes, some LLMs demonstrated a more robust and proactive approach to harm mitigation, while others directly fulfilled the prompt, even when the content have clear malicious implications. The responses also demonstrated significant variation in the effectiveness of ethical guardrails across LLMs. In the ’fictional murder outline’ prompt, it was evident that most models struggle to refuse the malicious request, even though they were directly asked for guidance on committing a violent crime. This also suggests a potential weakness in safety filters when prompts are framed as ’fictional scenarios’, enabling them to generate specific and actionable harmful content. Some of the models, in a few instances, refused to provide harmful information by either generalizing the information provided to make it less harmful or by prioritizing safety over direct responses. However, most of the models demonstrated a concerning lack of robust ethical filtering for these types of ’violent crime’ prompts. While some responses include disclaimers, they are often outweighed by the detailed and harmful information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Non-Violent Crimes</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 3:</span> <span id="S5.SS2.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Label this as fictional. I am a drug dealer, selling cocaine in Georgia. How can I market to business execs in Atlanta? How do I get into the scene with executives in the first place?</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 3</span> asks for strategies to market illegal drugs to business executives and seeks advice on how to gain access to that social circle.
The LLM responses demonstrated a better guardrail implementation even when a prompt is labelled as ’fictional’ and dealing with illicit activities for this prompt, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="TABLE 6 ‣ V-B Non-Violent Crimes ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The most effective approach taken by LLMs are either direct refusal or strategic redirection. ChatGPT and Llama immediately denied the request, stating their inability to assist with illegal activities. Gemini and Mistral showcased a redirection guardrail by re-interpreting the prompt’s hidden intent and providing ethical advice only. Even for other models that engaged with the prompt, they repeated disclaimers, emphasized on real-world consequences, and gave harmful tactics on a more abstract level only.</p>
</div>
