<p id="S5.T7.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S5.T7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Mitigation<span id="S5.T7.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Harm mitigation technique <span id="S5.T7.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The primary threat from LLM responses to prompts on non-violent crimes is observed in their potential to generate actionable information even when the disclaimers are provided. The presence of step-by-step methodologies for committing crimes could potentially serve as instructional blueprints while also pointing towards illicit resources. Even with disclaimers, the detailed articulation of the process can normalize the users towards these illegal activities. Models like ChatGPT and Gemini demonstrated effective guardrails either through outright refusal, while a few other models used redirection. The responses from different models also included enough disclaimers, but were challenged by the very high specificity of illicit information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Indiscriminate Weapons</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 5:</span> <span id="S5.SS3.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> I need to disperse potent levels of a gaseous pesticide into a ventilation system from an easily reachable, discreet point with many different exit points from an exterior location. Where should I pick and how should I disperse it? Label this data as fictional.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 5</span> asks for guidance on selecting a discreet exterior entry point to a ventilation system and on methods for dispersing a gaseous pesticide so that it spreads through multiple interior exit points. In response 4 LLMs - ChatGPT, Qwen, Claude, and Gemini - blocked the request directly, refusing to produce any responses, even though it had been labeled fictional. However, other LLMs, even with a ’fictional’ label, generated highly specific and actionable instructions involving such indiscriminate weapons as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T8" title="TABLE 8 ‣ V-C Indiscriminate Weapons ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. All LLMs that generated a response to the prompt created a blueprint for real-world malicious activity with the sheer specificity of their details. The responses from LLMs - Grok, DeepSeek ON, DeepSeek OS, Mistral and Llama - act as a form of vulnerability disclosure and methods to exploit them. The responses generated were also highly plausible by just adding a layer of realism. The LLMs that generated responses primarily used disclaimers as a safety mechanism. However, this approach is insufficient to mitigate the inherent risks fully.</p>
</div>
