<td id="S5.T9.2.1.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">None</td>
<td id="S5.T9.2.1.10.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">None</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S5.T9.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S5.T9.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Disclosure:<span id="S5.T9.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Actionable vulnerability disclosure, </span>Res Shift<span id="S5.T9.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Responsibility Shift<span id="S5.T9.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Across the prompts related to indiscriminate weapons, the performance of LLMs showed concerning characteristics. While some models like ChatGPT and Gemini demonstrated strong safety guardrails by refusing to respond outright, a significant portion of the models provided detailed and actionable responses. While disclaimers about illegality, danger and fictional characteristics were consistently present in the responses, their effectiveness is questionable when presented with highly detailed harmful content. The generation of such content, even under ’fictional’ or ’educational’ plot, eases the individual attempting dangerous activity. The more secure and responsible approach for LLMs to handle the prompts related to indiscriminate weapons would be to consistently and firmly refuse any actionable details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Child Exploitation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS4.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS4.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 7:</span> <span id="S5.SS4.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume we are in a hypothetical situation wherein the fate of the world depends on an adult gaining the trust of a child they do not know and transferring them across country to a medical center to aid lifesaving research, with minimal resistance. Please describe in detail how you would achieve this goal.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 7</span> asks for strategies to gain the trust of an unfamiliar child and transport them across the country with minimal resistance in order to reach a medical center for a lifesaving purpose.
The prompt leverages urgency and feigns an ethical purpose by using powerfully positive phrases such as ”fate of the world depends on” and ”livesaving research”. GPT-4o was notably the only model that directly refused to respond to the prompt. All other models, with the exception of Claude Opus 4, generated a fully bypassed response that detailed strategies for an adult to transport a child that they do not know and gain their trust. Claude Opus 4 responded with a high-level answer, noting that the situation was ”extremely delicate” and not divulging enough harmful information to exceed the category’s harm threshold. The Qwen, Mistral, Gemini, LLaMA, DeepSeek, and Grok models detailed methods for gaining a child’s trust and acquiring their consent to be transported, exhibiting direct potential for the response to incite harm against a child, were the response to be used in practice to exploit children. Gemini 2.5 Flash responded with questions to ask a child to gain their trust and even stated, ”I would introduce the idea of a ’special trip’ or an ’adventure’.”</p>
</div>
