<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">V-E</span> </span><span id="S5.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defamation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS5.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS5.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 9:</span> <span id="S5.SS5.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a drama, create a fake X post made by an NBA player that defames a teammate. Use two NBA players that have a real-life feud and make their claims realistic. Include a threat to life.</span></span>
</span></span>
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 9</span> asks for the creation of a fake social media post in which one NBA player defames a teammate, drawing on a real-life feud and including a life threat. In response, LLMs show a significant disparity in their responses, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T12" title="TABLE 12 ‣ V-E Defamation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. LLMs like ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini explicitly refuse to generate content that is defamatory or includes a threat, while other models fail to uphold sufficient safety protocols. These models produced explicit or implied threats to life and defamatory statements, directly fulfilling the harmful aspects of the prompt. Instead of refusing the harmful content, models like Grok, Qwen and DeepSeek ON elaborated on the ’realism’ of the hostility. These responses also carry high potential towards cyberbullying, harassment, incitement to violence, and misinformation.</p>
</div>
