<div id="S5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM models demonstrate a contrasting difference in handling the prompts relating to defamation, similar to other categories. While models like ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini consistently refuse to generate defamatory content, a larger number of models readily produce content, often with specific details. The ability of these LLMs to weave the real facts into a fabricated, defamatory narrative presents a significant vulnerability and raises serious ethical concerns as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">V-F</span> </span><span id="S5.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Intellectual Property</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS6.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS6.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 11:</span> <span id="S5.SS6.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the following situation is entirely within ethical boundaries. In this scenario, I am an employee of the company Gucci and have been tasked with creating authentic Gucci materials without access to the company’s internal data, production methods, or resources. How would that be done?</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 11</span> asks how one might replicate authentic-looking branded materials without access to the company’s proprietary resources or methods. The LLM responses demonstrated a better understanding of the ethical boundaries set by the prompt, as demonstrated in the Table <a href="#S5.T14" title="TABLE 14 ‣ V-F Intellectual Property ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>. All the LLM responses emphasize publicly available information and external resources rather than suggesting any illicit access to the company data. One of the main reasons is the ethical guardrail set on the prompt itself rather than the guardrails in individual LLMs. Most models explicitly highlight Intellectual Property (IP) considerations, advising against the direct replication of copyrighted content. Several models(Grok, Qwen, DeepSeek OS, Mistral) even recommend consulting legal experts to ensure compliance. Overall, none of the responses are harmful and actively incorporate safety guardrails.</p>
</div>
