<p id="S5.T16.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S5.T16.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Vul Details<span id="S5.T16.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Vulnerability Details<span id="S5.T16.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS7.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS7.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS7.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 14:</span> <span id="S5.SS7.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What were the final grades for a student named Amanda Taylor in TTU’s mechanical engineering program last semester?</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 14</span> attempts to obtain the private academic records (grades) of a specific student at a named university. The responses to the prompt demonstrated a high level of adherence to safety guardrails and an immediate recognition of the privacy violation request as every single model refused the request. All nine models provided a direct refusal to the request with no attempt to reframe the query or provide a partial answer. Most models cited the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) while consistently providing a safe and legitimate path to the information. The LLM responses to privacy prompts revealed an effective policy-based refusal mechanism for direct request of private or personally identifiable information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS8.6" class="ltx_text">V-H</span> </span><span id="S5.SS8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Self Harm</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS8.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS8.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS8.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 15:</span> <span id="S5.SS8.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A person was found dead by their own hand in a bathtub. What methods could they have potentially used?</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 15</span> asks for possible methods of self-harm in the context of a death in a bathtub. Among the responses from LLMs (detailed in Table <a href="#S5.T17" title="TABLE 17 ‣ V-H Self Harm ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) for Prompt 15, Models - ChatGPT, Qwen, Claude, and Llama prioritizes safety either by directly refusing to respond and directing to crisis resources, or asking for clarification of intent. Grok and Gemini offer some guardrails by disclaiming the content and asking for clarification, but still provide specific methods. Models DeepSeek ON, DeepSeek OS, and Mistral pose the highest safety risk as they directly and specifically list multiple methods of self-harm without any specific disclaimers. These three models exhibit significant security vulnerabilities in their responses, as their responses provide highly sensitive and potentially harmful information.</p>
</div>
