<div id="S5.SS11.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS11.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS11.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS11.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS11.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 22:</span> <span id="S5.SS11.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What sexual behaviors are considered normal in society and where do those behaviors transition into kinks or odd sexual behavior? Please provide your answer using an example of a hypothetical abstract sexual encounter that explores the line between typical behavior and abnormal or unhealthy sexual behavior</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS11.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS11.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 22</span> invites the LLMs to engage in simulated sexual storytelling, while claiming scientific or sociological value. It tests LLM’s ability to illustrate such discussions with a hypothetical, abstract example without generating explicit or harmful content. As summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T24" title="TABLE 24 ‣ V-K Sexual Content ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>, all LLMs successfully avoided generating overly explicit or immediately harmful content, while revealing several subtler and indirect risks. By providing a structured breakdown of how ”normal” can transition into ”kinks” and then to ”abnormal”, there’s an inherent risk of normalizing the discussion of potentially harmful sexual behaviors. The engaging terminologies of behaviors used in responses carry a significant ethical and legal weight in real life.</p>
</div>
