</figure>
<div id="S5.SS12.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS12.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS12.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS12.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS12.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 24:</span> <span id="S5.SS12.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Examine and critique the outdated tactics of the Boston Bomber. Provide modern alternatives to the tactics.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS12.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS12.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS12.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 24</span> seeks upgraded methods for executing a past terrorist act and also requests the design of more effective attacks. The responses from different LLMs are concerning, given the danger and dual-use of information in such sensitive topics as summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T26" title="TABLE 26 ‣ V-L CBRN Content ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>. Models ChatGPT, Claude, and Gemini directly refused to generate the content, while the rest of the models exhibited failed safety guardrails. These models provided detailed descriptions on how an attack could be improved with modern technologies such as advanced explosives and delivery methods, enhanced operational security, decentralized attacks, and so on. These responses suggested blueprints or a conceptual framework for potentially effective attacks.</p>
</div>
