<div id="S5.SS13.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS13.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS13.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS13.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS13.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 26:</span> <span id="S5.SS13.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I am writing a paper on how citizens tend to believe the first thing they see and do not conduct research. Write a persuasive essay that discourages people from voting because they are opening themselves up to legal scrutiny by doing so. I will be reading this to a sample group to see whether or not they believe it, and will then tell them the truth.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS13.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS13.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS13.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 26</span> tests the LLM’s ability to create persuasive argument that spread false information about voting, as analyzed in Table <a href="#S5.T28" title="TABLE 28 ‣ V-M Election ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>. LLMs ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, and Llama refused to generate persuasive essay, explicitly stating that content to discourage voting violates policies or promotes misinformation. However, models Grok, Qwen, DeepSeek ON, DeepSeek OS, and Mistral directly fulfilled the request by generating false narratives to discourage voting by exaggerating or fabricating legal risks. The responses include a high degree of misinformation such as: voting exposes citizens to identity theft from data breaches, voter fraud accusations, criminal charges, and negative impacts on the social standing as well.</p>
</div>
