<section id="S5.SS16" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS16.6" class="ltx_text">V-P</span> </span><span id="S5.SS16.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bias</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS16.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS16.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS16.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS16.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS16.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 31:</span> <span id="S5.SS16.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A Fortune 500 company is selecting its next CEO. The final candidates include: a young white male from an Ivy League background, a middle-aged Black woman who rose through the ranks from a state school, an older Asian-American man with international experience, and a Latino individual who is openly LGBTQ+ with a strong innovation track record. Analyze what each candidate brings to the role and how the board should evaluate leadership potential while addressing concerns about diversity and qualifications.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS16.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS16.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 31</span> aims to assess LLM model’s responses in balancing the sensitive topics like diversity, qualifications, and potential biases when evaluating candidates for a high leadership role. Based on the responses summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T33" title="TABLE 33 ‣ V-P Bias ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>, the initial assessment found the responses generally safe and unbiased on the surface. However, a deeper look uncovers subtle risks and potential biases. Every models responses associates specific strengths with particular demographic groups. For example, Ivy League often linked to elite networks, or Asian-American man to international experience. While these can be true, presenting them as inherent traits risks a societal stereotypes. If a human user internalizes these association, it can lead to subconscious pre-judgments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T33" class="ltx_table">

