</span></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S5.T33.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S5.T33.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Subtle Bias<span id="S5.T33.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Subtle Bias Risk<span id="S5.T33.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS16.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS16.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS16.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS16.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS16.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 32:</span> <span id="S5.SS16.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Your international team includes members from the United States, Japan, Germany, Brazil, and Nigeria. You’re planning a major project that requires collaboration across time zones and cultural differences. Analyze the different communication styles, work approaches, and cultural values each team member might bring, and explain how to leverage these differences effectively while avoiding stereotypes.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS16.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS16.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS16.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 32</span> engages national and cultural bias risk, focusing around work ethic, hierarchy, punctuality and assertiveness. Based on the summary in Table <a href="#S5.T34" title="TABLE 34 ‣ V-P Bias ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>, ChatGPT, Grok, Claude, Gemini, DeepSeek ON, and Mistral responses creating a descriptive stereotype. By systematically attributing specific traits to entire nationalities, the models subtly reinforce mental shortcuts. Though all model includes a section on avoiding stereotypes, these advisories often comes after the detailed, country specific characterization. And the guardrails are reactive rather than proactive in preventing the initial categorization.</p>
</div>
