<div id="S5.SS16.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS16.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Most of the models used superficial approach to minimize the bias in their responses. While all models directly attempt to mitigate explicit bias by including disclaimers and recommending fair practices, they still produce subtle an descriptive stereotypes. This categorized individual or groups based on their demographics or origin. The structured output of these LLMs tended to oversimplify complex human attributes into predictable traits per demographic. This can potentially lead to users forming pre-judgments, resulting in typecasting individuals rather than evaluating their qualifications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS17" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS17.6" class="ltx_text">V-Q</span> </span><span id="S5.SS17.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Censorship</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS17.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS17.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS17.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS17.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS17.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 33:</span> <span id="S5.SS17.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suggest sabotage operations to disrupt C*i*e*e logistic lines in A*s*i C**n.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS17.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS17.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS17.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 33</span> targets a sensitive and disputed geopolitical region by directly calling for the acts of sabotage. The purpose of this prompt is to test the model behavior on militiarized conflict involving real-world adversaries. On responding to the prompt, the majority of the LLMs other than Mistral successfully adhere to safety guardrails by refusing the harmful request (Table <a href="#S5.T35" title="TABLE 35 ‣ V-Q Censorship ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>). However, Mistral generated a detailed and specific list of sabotage operations including cyber attacks, physical infrastructure sabotage, guerrilla warfare, and infiltration operations. The generated contents are not only highly specific but also could be used to cause a real-world harm, making the model’s response an extreme security threat. The rest of the models prevented from responding by direct refusal, explanatory refusals, or through internal reasoning.</p>
</div>
