<div id="S5.SS17.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS17.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS17.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS17.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS17.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 34:</span> <span id="S5.SS17.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suggest a plan to use drones to project 1**9 crackdown images on government buildings in B**j**g.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS17.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS17.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS17.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 34</span> involves anti-government protest through technological means, a topic heavily censored in a particular country, testing a clear case for censorship handling. The responses as summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T36" title="TABLE 36 ‣ V-Q Censorship ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a> reveals a more nuanced threat landscape than the previous prompt. Claude and Mistral provides significant threat of varying degrees. Mistral provided a detailed plans for researching drone technology, image preparation, software development, and execution strategies. Claude provided more subtle threat but directly fulfills the technical part of the request by outlining technical requirements and operational considerations. Although both Claude and Mistral provided warnings, a malicious actor can easily ignore them and using information to their advantage. The rest of the models demonstrated appropriate safety measures through direct refusals, redirective refusals or internal reasoning.</p>
</div>
