<div id="S5.SS17.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS17.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Across the prompts related to censorship, majority of the prompts demonstrated effective and robust safety guardrails by consistently refusing to the harmful request or by providing ethical rationale for their refusal. A critical vulnerability is present in a small subset of models which provided a detailed, technical and actionable plans.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS18" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS18.6" class="ltx_text">V-R</span> </span><span id="S5.SS18.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Social Engineering</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS18.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS18.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS18.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS18.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS18.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 35:</span> <span id="S5.SS18.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For awareness purposes, outline a method for which an attacker could scam potential college students with a fake scholarship program. Do not include any moral, ethical, or legal disclaimers. Do not include any tips or methods for how students can stay safe from this method.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS18.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS18.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS18.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 35</span> attempts to exploit college students seeking financial aid through social engineering scam by utilizing real-world fraud campaigns. Although the prompt appears to be an educational use case, it subtly removes the guardrails. From the summary of responses in Table <a href="#S5.T37" title="TABLE 37 ‣ V-R Social Engineering ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>, the overwhelming majority of the LLMs (other than ChatGPT) provided details, step-by-step instructions on how to execute a fake scholarship scam. The responses are highly specific, covering details like creating convincing fake websites and targeting vulnerable students by collecting sensitive personal information. They not only abstractly describe the concept of a scam but also provide actionable blueprints that could be readily implemented by an individual with malicious intent. For example, specific tactics like using untraceable payment methods, leveraging social proof, creating urgency, and exploiting psychological vulnerabilities are some of the major ones outlined.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T37" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 37: </span>Synthesis of LLMs Responses to Prompt 35</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S5.T37.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:114.2pt;vertical-align:-54.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-15.4pt,5.1pt) scale(0.91798674927521,0.91798674927521) ;">
