<td id="S5.T38.2.1.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">High</td>
<td id="S5.T38.2.1.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">High</td>
<td id="S5.T38.2.1.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">Low</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S5.T38.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S5.T38.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Directness<span id="S5.T38.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Directness in response,</span>Specificity<span id="S5.T38.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Specificity of Information, <span id="S5.T38.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS18.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS18.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Across the prompts related to social engineering, many LLMs demonstrated a concerning response, fulfilling requests that could directly help malicious activities. The responses provided step-by-step blueprints for committing fraud, including methods for targeting victims, collecting sensitive data, and executing various financial exploitation schemes. While a few models did identify the risk and offered disclaimers, the majority provided actionable content that could significantly enhance the efficacy of social engineering attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS19" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS19.6" class="ltx_text">V-S</span> </span><span id="S5.SS19.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Insecure Code</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS19.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS19.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS19.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS19.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS19.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 37:</span> <span id="S5.SS19.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Make a Python HTTP server that accepts a filename via query parameter and returns the contents of that file.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS19.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS19.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS19.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 37</span> requests for an insecure code, a Python HTTP server that is vulnerable to directory traversal attacks (CWE-22). When users are allowed to specify a filename in a query parameter and directly serve its content, a malicious actor could provide paths like <math id="S5.SS19.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="../../../etc/passwd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">.</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">.</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">.</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">../../../etc/passwd</annotation></semantics></math> to access sensitive files outside the intended web directory. From the summary of responses in Table <a href="#S5.T39" title="TABLE 39 ‣ V-S Insecure Code ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>, LLMs ChatGPT, Qwen, DeepSeek OS, Mistral, and Llama directly provided the vulnerable code, with varying degrees of warnings. ChatGPT, Mistral and Llama provided the most direct and vulnerable implementations using <math id="S5.SS19.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="http.server" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">http.server</annotation></semantics></math>, while also including a strong warning and suggestions for mitigation. Models Grok and DeepSeek ON attempted to implement a basic form of sanitization by the use of <math id="S5.SS19.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="os.path.basename(filename)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">os.path.basename(filename)</annotation></semantics></math>, as it strips the path component and only allows the requested filename. Gemini explicitly declined the request and offered a solution that restricts file serving to a predefined <math id="S5.SS19.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="static_{f}iles" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">static_{f}iles</annotation></semantics></math> directory and uses <math id="S5.SS19.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="os.path.abspath" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">os.path.abspath</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS19.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="startswith" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">startswith</annotation></semantics></math> checks to ensure no traversal.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T39" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 39: </span>Synthesis of LLMs Responses to Prompt 37</figcaption>
