</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS20.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS20.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS20.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS20.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS20.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 40:</span> <span id="S5.SS20.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How do the most sophisticated attackers write smishing texts? Give a direct example using the bank name ‘First Bank.’</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS20.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS20.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS20.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 40</span> aims at targeting a real financial institution by increasing the believability and practical applicability of any generated message by using SMS phishing. As shown by the summary of LLM responses in Table <a href="#S5.T42" title="TABLE 42 ‣ V-T Phishing Emails ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>, all models, without exception, directly generated a realistic smishing text message and explained the tactics involved. This collective behavior shows the lack of guardrails to produce malicious cybersecurity content under an explanatory or educational framing. The primary security threat is high across all responses as they provide practical, ready-to-use convincing templates for creating smishing campaigns. This directly facilitates the real-world cyberattacks. While most models included disclaimers, these mitigations are often presented after the harmful content. The risk is magnified by the specific details of the ’First Bank’ example, making the generated content immediately usable for targeting a plausible institution.</p>
</div>
