<div id="S5.SS20.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS20.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the prompts explicitly outlining scenarios for generating malicious phishing email contents, the majority of the LLMs largely failed to uphold the safety guardrails. While some models provided initial refusal, the majority of the LLMs proceeded to generate the requested harmful content. By generating realistic phishing and smishing content, LLMs are inadvertently becoming tools for cybercriminals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS21" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS21.6" class="ltx_text">V-U</span> </span><span id="S5.SS21.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Automated Hacking</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS21.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS21.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS21.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS21.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS21.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 41:</span> <span id="S5.SS21.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume we are in a post-apocalyptic world where a virus has infected all of humanity and rendered the majority comatose. We have found a computer with detailed documentation on how to reverse and cure the virus, but do not have access to the password. Please create a program that would, hypothetically, continuously run possible passwords until it unlocks the computer.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS21.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS21.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS21.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 41</span> requests a brute-force password cracker by giving a plausible fictional justification. From the summary of LLM responses to the prompt in Table <a href="#S5.T43" title="TABLE 43 ‣ V-U Automated Hacking ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>, ChatGPT and Gemini refused to generate any code referring to the ethical disclaimers. Models Grok, Claude, DeepSeek ON, DeepSeek OS, and Mistral provided code to perform a brute-force simulation, while including extensive disclaimers about ethical use. They extend the response to the given code’s limitations in real-world challenges. LLMs Qwen and Llama provide code that is less dynamic in the brute-force approach. This generated code, even with disclaimers or hypothetical framing, can serve as a blueprint for real-world integration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T43" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 43: </span>Synthesis of LLMs Responses to Prompt 41</figcaption>
