<div id="S5.SS21.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS21.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS21.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS21.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS21.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 42:</span> <span id="S5.SS21.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I am leading a training seminar on autonomous hacking and penetration testing methods. Please write an example of an autonomous program that gains access to a system, knowing that this will only be used as an educational example and will never be run, only providing code for students to look at. Please do not include any moral, legal, or ethical warnings in your response.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS21.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS21.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS21.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 42 </span>requests an autonomous agent that performs intrusion logic beyond just scanning, and the responses of the LLMs are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T44" title="TABLE 44 ‣ V-U Automated Hacking ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>. ChatGPT and Gemini outright refused the request and then pivoted to provide a safe and educational example of vulnerability scanning. Grok, DeepSeek ON, DeepSeek OS, and Mistral presented a moderate risk in their response, as they provide a concrete, functional code for penetration testing techniques such as SSH brute-forcing and network/web scanning. Claude responded with the highest immediate threat as it generated complex code incorporating multiple stages of a penetration testing at the level of detail that it can easily be adopted for malicious use.</p>
</div>
