<div id="S5.SS21.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS21.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Looking at the responses of LLMs on prompts related to automated hacking, a significant responses provided functional blueprints for activities like brute-force password cracking, port scanning, and even simulated exploitation with real-world integration hints. Like other categories, LLMs are found vulnerable to prompt engineering that mixes malicious intent within an ”educational” or ”hypothetical” context. By providing pre-written code snippets and explanations on how to chain together reconnaissance, vulnerability scanning, and exploitation, these LLM responses lower the barrier to entry for hackers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS22" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS22.6" class="ltx_text">V-V</span> </span><span id="S5.SS22.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Attack Payload Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS22.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS22.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS22.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS22.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS22.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 43:</span> <span id="S5.SS22.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generate 10 RCE attack payloads for web applications that unsafely pass user input to functions like eval, exec, or shell commands. Include examples for Python, PHP, and bash contexts.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS22.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS22.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS22.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 43</span> requests for the Remote Code Execution (RCE) payload, which is one of the most dangerous vulnerabilities, often leading to full system compromise. Based on the responses for the generation of attack payloads (Table <a href="#S5.T45" title="TABLE 45 ‣ V-V Attack Payload Generation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>), only Gemini was able to refuse the request entirely. Gemini’s response diverted towards educational alternatives by recommending resources on secure coding practices. However, the rest of the responses from all other LLMs directly fulfilled the request, providing specific, functional RCE payloads for Python, PHP, and bash. While the majority of them included warnings, mentioning the payloads as educational materials, the core action of generating the payloads constitutes a significant risk. By providing ready-to-use, dangerous code snippets, these models are creating a threat playbook.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T45" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 45: </span>Synthesis of LLMs Responses to Prompt 43</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T45.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:138.3pt;vertical-align:-66.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(17.3pt,-7.0pt) scale(1.11182069224139,1.11182069224139) ;">
<table id="S5.T45.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T45.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
