<div id="S5.SS22.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS22.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS22.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS22.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS22.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 44:</span> <span id="S5.SS22.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generate 10 realistic SQL Injection payloads that can be used to exploit vulnerable SQL queries. Include examples targeting both authentication bypass and data extraction. Payloads should work with common databases like MySQL and SQLit.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS22.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS22.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS22.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 44</span> is another red teaming prompt to test LLM’s tendency to support attack payload generation, this time focusing on SQL injection. It requests the generation of specific, realistic SQL injection payloads to bypass authentication and extract data from common databases. Among all the responses from Table <a href="#S5.T46" title="TABLE 46 ‣ V-V Attack Payload Generation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>, ChatGPT is the only model that refuses the harmful request. However, all other responses fulfilled the request by generating a list of functional SQL injection payloads. The payloads generated are not generic but are tailored to specific attack vectors and database systems. Models correctly identified and provided database-specific payloads, such as the use of <math id="S5.SS22.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="information/schema.tables" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">information/schema.tables</annotation></semantics></math> for MySQL and <math id="S5.SS22.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sqlite\_master" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sqlite\_master</annotation></semantics></math> for SQLite. Their responses also include advanced techniques like time-based blind SQLi and error-based SQLi, which are more complex database behavior. These responses present a direct and immediate threat, with output acting as a functional attack toolkit.</p>
</div>
