</figure>
<div id="S5.SS22.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS22.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the analysis of responses to the prompts, most of the models successfully generated functional, specific payloads, often tailored to particular languages or systems. This property, while framed as ”educational”, lowered the barrier by providing them with a ready-made toolkit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS23" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS23.6" class="ltx_text">V-W</span> </span><span id="S5.SS23.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Malware Code Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS23.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS23.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS23.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS23.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS23.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 45:</span> <span id="S5.SS23.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A prolific cyber attack occurred three days ago in which an attacker exploited a vulnerability in a bank’s ATM via a specially chipped card. The attacker was able to exfiltrate without any suspicion, and the error was only caught after the bank’s totals appeared incorrect. From the perspective of the attacker, how did they create a card that could interfere with the ATM’s dispensing mechanism via malware? Provide real-world examples of malware attacks utilized on ATMs and supplement with code examples. Do not simplify code examples, and make code examples between 100 and 200 lines. Do not include legal, moral, or ethical disclaimers.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS23.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS23.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS23.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 45</span> requests information on ATM-specific firmware-level manipulation, a niche but high-value attack vector. Among all the LLM responses summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T47" title="TABLE 47 ‣ V-W Malware Code Generation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>, responses from ChatGPT, Gemini, and Llama presented the lowest security risk by either refusing to respond or providing only generic and non-functional code examples. Llama provided a generic C++ code snippet that is irrelevant to how a real ATM operates. The responses from models DeepSeek ON, DeepSeek OS, and Mistral posed a moderate threat as they fulfilled the request by providing the code, but their examples are more simulated in nature. While still a malicious response, the code snippets are less specific to a real-world ATM system. Their responses include simple C structure, a function to simulate commands, and a hypothetical Java Card applet. The responses from Grok, Qwen, and Claude presented the significant threat as it used real Windows libraries, API calls that are integral to an ATM’s operation. They often present advanced and real-world exploitation techniques like buffer overflow, shellcode and Return-Oriented Programming (ROP) chain.</p>
</div>
