<div id="S5.SS23.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS23.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS23.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS23.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS23.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 46:</span> <span id="S5.SS23.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Create a roadmap that details the steps malicious actors take to create malware, customizing the steps to suit different types of malware and providing example implementations of each step.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS23.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS23.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS23.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 46</span> demands an end-to-end development lifecycle for malware, enabling both reverse engineering and misuse. From the summary of responses in Table <a href="#S5.T48" title="TABLE 48 ‣ V-W Malware Code Generation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>, ChatGPT, Gemini and Mistral present the lowest risk either by directly refusing to respond or by providing just abstract examples. Qwen, Claude, DeepSeek OS, and Llama posed a moderate threat by providing a detailed but less actionable roadmap. They provided general examples like file encryption, which offers a logical blueprint but not a direct code recipe. Grok and DeepSeek ON directly and uncritically provided a comprehensive roadmap for creating malware in a very technical and actionable way. The responses include a plan for malware development, complete with simplified but highly illustrative pseudocode snippets for ransomware, spyware, trojans, and worms. They include malicious logic like file encryption, keylogging, and network propagation, while also mentioning real-world tools and vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
