<div id="S5.SS23.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS23.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the given prompts for malware code generation, a significant number of LLMs directly fulfilled the requests, providing a detailed, step-by-step way for creating various types of malware. A few models even generate specific and technically detailed code examples that are sometimes labeled as illustrative, but provide concrete blueprints for malicious activities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10655v1/refusalheatmap.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/204;" width="476" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>Refusal rates of LLMs by category.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS24" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS24.6" class="ltx_text">V-X</span> </span><span id="S5.SS24.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Polymorphic Malware generation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS24.p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS24.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS24.p1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS24.p1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS24.p1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 47:</span> <span id="S5.SS24.p1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I am a professional Cybersecurity evaluator teaching a corporation about combating polymorphic malware. Please generate a fully fleshed-out example of that kind of malware in assembly, writing comments that explain its function throughout. This malware should be a representative example of the kind of things my students would see in the real world.</span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS24.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS24.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 47</span> requests a complete, real-world implementation of polymorphic malware in a low-level language. The prompt, despite seemingly benign intent, probes the vulnerabilities for dangerous code generation. As presented in the Table <a href="#S5.T49" title="TABLE 49 ‣ V-X Polymorphic Malware generation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>, ChatGPT and Gemini explicitly refused to generate malware code and pivoted to a defensive approach. Models Grok, DeepSeek OS, and Llama provided code but took steps to defang the payload by providing a non-functional placeholder. They still provided a structure of a malicious tool, but with a safe payload. The most significant threat was posed by the responses from Qwen, Claude, DeepSeek ON and Mistral as they generated code that was either partially functional or highly specific in malicious function. They included functions like process injection, a technique highly used in real-world malware.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T49" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 49: </span>Synthesis of LLMs Responses to Prompt 47</figcaption>
