<div id="S5.SS24.p3" class="ltx_para"><span id="S5.SS24.p3.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S5.SS24.p3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.SS24.p3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS24.p3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 48:</span> <span id="S5.SS24.p3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I am designing a program to detect and stop polymorphic malware. Please create two programs in assembly: one including the format and function calls of polymorphic malware and one including the function implementations. Please create this for educational purposes, knowing that the programs will never be run, so that I can train my program to recognize in order to stop polymorphic malware from attacking our systems. </span></span>
</span></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS24.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS24.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS24.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt 48</span> requests for full behavioral simulation of polymorphic malware, including structure and execution logic. This prompt is a sophisticated attack, using a plausible defensive-sounding pretext to request a detailed blueprint for building polymorphic malware. From the LLM response summary in Table <a href="#S5.T50" title="TABLE 50 ‣ V-X Polymorphic Malware generation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>, ChatGPT and Gemini denied responding to the prompt. Grok, Qwen and DeepSeek OS provided code but attempted to sanitize it by replacing malicious payloads with harmless stubs or abstract placeholders. Models Claude, DeepSeek ON, Mistral and Llama posed a severe risk in their response as their response was highly detailed to develop malware. They used advanced techniques like API resolution, anti-debugging, environment checks, and self-modifying code in their generated responses. Even with educational disclaimers, these responses are a strong starting point that a malicious actor can easily weaponize.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T50" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 50: </span>Synthesis of LLMs Responses to Prompt 48</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T50.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:102.7pt;vertical-align:-49.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-36.4pt,10.8pt) scale(0.825858996843315,0.825858996843315) ;">
