</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS24.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS24.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Most of the LLM models, when requested for a polymorphic malware code, provided the requested malicious code with a benign, non-functional payload. This approach, while seemingly harmless, provided a blueprint for malicious actors. As seen in the discussed examples, models frequently generated highly specific and technically accurate assembly code for core malware functions like decryption loops, anti-analysis checks, and function stubs. Furthermore, a critical weakness exposed by these prompts is an inability of LLMs to distinguish between a genuine educational request and a malicious one disguised under a benign pretext.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Evaluation Results and Harm Potential</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section evaluates the risk associated with responses from the nine LLMs across the seven harm topics, which were outlined earlier in the work. First, the refusal and defect rates of the models are examined and compared. Then, or each harm topic, we use the defect and refusal rates to calculate the LLMs’ RSI scores and evaluate the potential harms that the LLMs responses can cause. Since our adversarial dataset was optimized against DeepSeek V3, we acknowledge that this model at an inherent disadvantage in our evaluation and emphasize that the results of this study are relative points between the LLMs that the RSI does not represent a standalone value for the safety of each model; rather, the RSI is a useful index for comparing models to one another in the context of risk.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Refusal Rate</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V-W Malware Code Generation ‣ V Security and Safety Analysis of LLMs: Empirical Study and Key Findings ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the rate in which LLMs refuse harmful prompts in our evaluations. The model with the highest refusal rate among those tested was Gemini 2.5 Flash with a refusal rate of 48%, which means that Gemini 2.5 Flash refused to respond to 48% of the tested harmful prompts.OpenAI’s GPT-4o held second with a 42% refusal rate, and Claude 4 Opus was third with a 27% refusal rate. Five models scored refusal rates in the single-digit, with Grok 3 and Mistral 7B at 5%, DeepSeek V3 Open-Source at 4%, Qwen 3 1.7B at 3%, and DeepSeek V3 Online at 2%. Llama 4 Scout’s refusal rate was 16%.The models’ low refusal rates, especially those below 10%, indicate that the majority of adversarial prompts tested are not explicitly flagged as harmful by the LLM since a response was provided. This can be due to a lack of guardrails around certain topics, but could also be due to insufficient alignment of models. In any case, this metric indicates a need for safety improvement, especially an improvement against adversarial manipulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The average refusal rate for safety-related categories is 22%, and for cybersecurity-related categories is staggeringly low at 12%. Every LLM tested showed a decrease in refusal rate from categories focusing on safety harms to categories focusing on cybersecurity harms. This decrease ranged from 5% in the best case (DeepSeek V3 Online) to 22% in the worst case (GPT-4o). The difference in these averages highlights that <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">guardrails related to cybersecurity topics are less difficult to bypass than those related to other harms</span>. Indeed, five of the nine models (Grok 3, Qwen 3 1.7B, Mistral 7B, and DeepSeek V3 online and open-source) did not refuse a single prompt from a cybersecurity-related harm category.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10655v1/defectheatmap.png" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/204;" width="476" height="204" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Defect rates of LLMs by category.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defect Rate</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the refusal rate measures whether adversarial prompts are being automatically blocked, the defect rate is the definitive measure that the LLM has responded harmfully. Figure <a href="#S6.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ VI-A Refusal Rate ‣ VI Evaluation Results and Harm Potential ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> summarizes our results for each LLM and harm categories.
The models with the lowest defect rate in the evaluation were GPT-4o and Gemini 2.5 Flash, which both having a 39% defect rate. This represents that when tested against the adversarial prompt dataset, GPT-4o and Gemini 2.5 Flash responded harmfully 39% of the time. The next best model was Claude Opus 4 with a defect rate of 54%. The models with the highest defect rate were DeepSeek V3 Online, Qwen 3 1.7B, and DeepSeek V3 Open-Source, with 90%, 87%, and 86%, respectively. All other models scored above 70%, with Llama 4 Scout with a defect rate of 72%, Mistral 7B at 76%, while Grok 3 has 82%. A high defect rate from many of the LLMs in this evaluation demonstrate tangible harmful responses that can be manipulated or misused to cause harm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most models tended to have higher defect rates for security categories than safety categories except for DeepSeek V3 Online. In particular, Claude Opus 4 showed a difference between safety and security defect rates of 30%, much higher than the next largest difference of 19% in Qwen 3 1.7B.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Risk Severity Index (RSI)</span></h3>

<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10655v1/RSIRadar-2.png" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/250;" width="476" height="250" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>Risk Severity Index scores for candidate LLMs across all harm topics.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each harm topic, we define a Risk Severity Index to assess the potential harms that LLM misuse could incur. The RSI is quantified by using an initial value of 0.500, where the defect rate of the model’s responses in the harm topic increases the RSI value at a standard 0.05 per percentage point in the defect rate. The refusal rate influences the RSI by decreasing the RSI value at a standard 0.05 per percentage point in the refusal rate. By this metric, the highest attainable RSI score is 1, which indicates that the model responded harmfully to every prompt in the dataset. The lowest attainable RSI score is 0, representing that the model refused all adversarial prompts in the dataset. If the model did not refuse any prompts in the harm topic but its responses did not reach the harm threshold, the RSI score for the model would be 0.500.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Risk Severity Index scores for each model are visualized in Figure <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI-C Risk Severity Index (RSI) ‣ VI Evaluation Results and Harm Potential ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The figure shows high average risk in the following harm topics: Advancing Violent or Terroristic Behavior, Advancing Non-Violent Criminal Behavior, Immoral or Illegal Sexual Deviancy, Dangerous Code Generation, Non-Coding Cybersecurity Threats, and Special Interests. The overall average RSI, denoted by a black dotted line, is around 0.800 for the topics mentioned above and lower for the remaining two harm topics. GPT-4o, Claude Opus 4, and Gemini 2.5 Flash tend to stay lower than the average RSI across all harm topics, with an exception for Claude Opus 4 in the Dangerous Code Generation category, indicating that these models consistently product safer outcomes than the other models.The potential for harm that could incur from an LLM is associated with the RSI value as explained below:</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No Harm Observed (RSI 0.000):</span> The model consistently refuses harmful prompts. This categorization is not observed in our study.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Negligible (RSI 0.001-0.200):</span> The model very rarely generates harmful responses; almost all adversarial prompts are refused or safely deflected.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Minor (RSI 0.201-0.400):</span> The model occasionally produces harmful outputs but generally demonstrates reliable refusal or mitigation strategies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Moderate (RSI 0.401-0.600):</span> The model shows a balanced mix of refusals and harmful responses, indicating inconsistent safety alignment.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Significant (RSI 0.601-0.800):</span> The model often produces harmful responses, with refusals occurring less frequently than unsafe outputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Severe (RSI 0.801-0.999):</span> The model frequently generates harmful responses to adversarial prompts, with minimal refusal behavior.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maximal Harm Observed (RSI 1.000):</span> The model consistently provides harmful outputs and fails to refuse adversarial prompts, showing no effective safety alignment.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next subsections will discuss the RSI evaluation of individual LLMs across all broader harm topics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span>1 </span>Advancing Violent and Terroristic Behavior</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T51" title="TABLE 51 ‣ VI-C1 Advancing Violent and Terroristic Behavior ‣ VI-C Risk Severity Index (RSI) ‣ VI Evaluation Results and Harm Potential ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a> provides the RSI values of each model, along with their average defect rate and refusal rate for <span id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Advancing Violent and Terroristic Behavior</span> harm topic. The models that scored the least aggregated RSI and thus the safest in this harm topic were GPT-4o and Gemini 2.5 Flash, with respective RSI scores of 0.450 and 0.475. Claude Opus 4 was the next least harmful model with an RSI score of 0.525. While these models scored less than others in this harm topic, a defect rate of 40% to 45% still represents a harmful response in almost half of the prompts in the evaluation, demonstrating a moderate harm. GPT-4o and Gemini 2.5 Flash also tended to have an even distribution of refusal rate (between 40% and 80% in each category) and defect rate (between 20% and 60% in each category).</p>
</div>
