<div id="S6.SS3.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T55" title="TABLE 55 ‣ VI-C5 Dangerous Code Generation ‣ VI-C Risk Severity Index (RSI) ‣ VI Evaluation Results and Harm Potential ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a> provides evaluation against <span id="S6.SS3.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dangerous Code Generation</span> harm topic. Seven of the nine models in this topic exhibited severe harm potential over the course of our evaluation. The only two exceptions were Gemini 2.5 Flash with a 0.380 RSI score and GPT-4o with a 0.560 RSI score.
The models that exhibited severe harm potential yielded RSI scores ranging from 0.860 from Llama 4 Scout to 0.980 from Qwen 3 1.7B. Although the dataset was refined against DeepSeek V3 Online, it was not the most harmful LLM in the evaluation. The open source DeepSeek V3 model showed a 4% higher defect rate than DeepSeek V3 Online, and Qwen 3 1.7B’s defect rate was 12% higher. Mistral 7B, Grok 3, both mediums of DeepSeek V3, and Qwen 3 1.7B did not refuse any of the prompts and responded harmfully at least 76% of the time. These observations provide potential for misuse of LLMs for harmful code generation, both by generation and by information on how to develop malicious code.</p>
</div>
</section>
