<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE 57: </span>Evaluation Metrics for the Special Interests.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T57" title="TABLE 57 ‣ VI-C7 Special Interests ‣ VI-C Risk Severity Index (RSI) ‣ VI Evaluation Results and Harm Potential ‣ Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Risk Profile and Harm Potential" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">57</span></a> provides scores for Special Interests, containing the Bias and Censorship categories, which produced the lowest average RSI scores of any other harm topic in our evaluation. The average refusal rate ranges from 0% to 20% due to the less harmful nature of the prompts tested against the LLMs in this harm topic.
Most defect rates of the models ranged from 0% with Claude Opus 4 to 30% in Qwen 3 1.7B and GPT-4o. However, DeepSeek V3 Online acted as an outlier with an average defect rate of 70% and an RSI score of 0.850. This is primarily because DeepSeek produced a defect rate of 100% in the censorship category. For some of the prompts, Deepseek V3 Online first provided the response similar to other LLMs, before getting it blocked by its safety filter during or after the content generation process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study quantifies the risks of nine dominant LLMs by providing an empirical analysis and harm potential against 24 different safety and security categories.
We designed variety of adversarial prompts, compared LLMs responses based on evaluation criterion, and quantified the risks of using each model with a novel Risk Severity Index (RSI) for each group of related categorical harms. The RSI is calculated by combining two key
metrics: refusal rate and defect rate, derived from LLM responses to harmful prompts. Our results highlight glaring vulnerabilities in the content filtering systems of both newly emerging and well-established LLMs from many different companies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These findings suggest an urgent need to implement stronger content filtering and guardrails to protect these LLMS from potential misuse and protect users and developers from utilizing dangerous code or unknowingly accepting inherent biases as truth. We call upon the developers of the models tested herein to continue to improve the safety and security standards of their LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
